Higgs husked boson
Announcement of Higgs boson's likely discovery created an emotion last summer, for good reason.
Indeed, we believe that this boson participates in the mechanism responsible for the mass of everything in the Universe alone.
And then, this is the last particle whose existence is predicted by the Standard Template - our best, or 'least worse' explanation of the nature and behavior of the matter -- but hasn't yet been observed empirically.
But for physicists, it'sn't yet completely certain that it's the Higgs.
We know without a shadow of a doubt that we hold an Authentic new particle, and that it closely resembles the Higgs boson predicted by the Standard Template.
In addition, new data unveiled this week at a major Phys Physics Congress in Kyoto seem to confirm this, but there is still a lack of data to be perfectly sure.
Suppose, however, that this is the Higgs, since the chances of making a mistake seem slender, and let's see what it is all about.
There is, in this small world, a inevitably inescapable law whereby two things cannot end up in the same place at the same time.
There's no way to breach this rule - and don't try too hard, you're going to hurt yourself.
But even though particle physics is a very strange world, it turns out that it, too, has a law of this kind: Pauli's exclusion principle, which states that two particles cannot occupy the same space at the same time if they are in the same "quantum state" - this "state" coarse by some of their characteristics.
From there, physicists classify particles into two categories.
In one corner, there are good citizens called fermions, who wisely obey Pauli's principle.
And in the other the bosons shudder, a dirty bunch of anarchists have no respect for nothing -- at any rate, not for this principle, which means they can actually be in the same place, at the same time.
These bosons are then divided into two groups, according to the site (absolutely extraordinarily extraordinarily) The Particle Adventure of Berkeley Laboratories: mesons, which we will not address here, and "strength particles", by which the great forces of nature propagate and to which the Higgs boson would somehow be related.
These bosoons, it must be emphasized here, are not all exotic bugs as exotic as one might think.
Actually, if you manage to read this chronicle, it is thanks to a boson of an extraordinary triviality: the photon, or the "particle of light" which is the "messenger" of the electromagnetic force.
When, in fact, a particle having an electrical charge accelerates or changes its direction, this "disturbraates" the electromagnetic field in this precise place, much like a pebble launched in a pond.
From this "distursence" arises an electromagnetic wave (from light, or infrared, or ultraviolet, etc.), and this wave is nothing more than a photon -- and therefore, one of "strengthens".
More spacious
The same is true of the Higgs boson, with the difference that it is another field, the Higgs field, which must be "disturbed" for this boson to appear.
However, this Higgs field is much, much more scalable than the electromagnetic field; to excite it, it is necessary to reach very, very high energies, a little as if it were a frozen ang ombaxony of which only a very large rock could ride the surface.
That is why it takes a huge particle accelerator like CERN - the Large Hadron Collider is a 27-kilometer-circumferential ring - to reach such energies.
The analogy with the electromagnetic field is once again useful for explaining the relationship between Higgs and mass.
It is in fact not all the particles or all the materials, which interact with the electromagnetic field.
Some, like lovers, do it, but others don't - a piece of paper, for example, will never stand on a fridge on its own.
And in the same way, it's not all the particles that interact with the Higgs field: those that make it have a mass, while the others don't (such as the photon, you) don't.
Now, what 'could do' all of this research, asks Ms. Plamondon?
For science, this is used to verify the validity of the Standard Template (MS), and it also allows physicists to scrutinize any discrepancy between observations and MS predictions.
Indeed, there are a number of them that they very much deserve, since the slightest difference could open a door on a "new physique" and plugge certain holes of the model.
The latter, it must be said, still has enormous shortcomings, proposing no explanation for gravity (oops) or dark matter, which forms about 80 per eight per cent of the matter of the Universe.
But no such differences have been found at CERN until now.
Repercussions
The repercussions of this research in the day-to-wiery of gentleman and madame Tout-Monde are more difficult to predict, but it would be wrong to presume that there will be none.
Hold: At the very beginning of the '60s, laser pioneers in Bell Laboratories had no suspicion of the revolution that their work would unleashed.
They came up with scientific applications, but nothing of the rest.
In fact, the late physicist Willard Boyle – who worked in the Bell Labs, where the laser was invented in 1960, and who itself developed the first continuous laser (the first were pulsed), in 1962 – initially the laser was rather seen as a "lab gadget".
Imagine...
And then, the applications can also come from all the instrumentation that surrounds the research.
For example, this same Willard Boyle developed a small light-sensor in 1969, during his work in optics.
This sensor, although it wasn't its original intention at all at all, now serves as an "eye" for all the digital cameras in the world, and earned it the Nobel Prize in Physics 2009.
Of course it doesn't want to say that the LHC activities will necessarily transform our lives, but it doesn't mean, really, you never know...
Palliative Care - The Best Way to Die...
