Prosprostate cancer that gnawed at him had given him a respite for two years.
"They had given me five years to live, I made seven years," he said, half-fig half-grapes, lying in his bed at the Victor-Gadbois palliative health-care house of Beloeil, where he arrived the day before.
"But it's always a shock, we can't be prepared for that," he said.
The disease does its work: an enormous weakness that prevents it from going to the toilet alone, and even eating alone.
Situated before an appetizing lunch, he agrees to be helped to eat, remarried.
Courage, he even manages to smile, talks to the strangers who are busy around him, bring him his medicines, offer him a bath.
The Courage of Ordinary Death.
"My biggest wish is to be cured of diarrhoeal, it's humiliating," he said.
A few hours later, the team found a cure for this evil.
"In the course of our life, we learn that a man piss standing up," says Pierre Brodeur, psychologist at Victor-Gadbois.
Regressing at the child stage for some people is an unacceptable humiliation.
'It depends on the ability of the person to accept reshuffles,' he says.
Because, in the opinion of several people working in palliative care, great moments are taking place at the very heart of this regression.
Patients who enter the Victor-Gadbois palliative care home all suffer from cancer.
They have a lifelong prognosis of up to three months.
At this stage, "team of doctors and nurses who surround them no longer provide so-called "curative care".
For Ms. A, 89 years old, the worst fear is dying “conscious and suffocated”.
But the disease made me discover my children.
"I have good children," she added.
"I don't have any more wishes in my life," she said, before she could be given a mask to help him breathe.
In the next few days, she is still expecting a final visit by her son from Italy.
At Victor-Gadbois, with a group of volunteers, body care and food assistance were provided with a group of volunteers.
Palliative care, given when there's nothing else to do.
To make death more comfortable.
In Quebec, there is one bed of palliative care for 11,700 inhabitants.
It's very little when you know we all end up dying one day.
Here, life continues in the best possible conditions, explains Dr Christiane Martel, one of the doctors in the house.
Whether it's physical, emotive or spiritual comfort.
The driddy person will be accepted to provide spire or Pepsi treatment, depending on the request.
Diabetics will no longer be required to control their sugar levels.
And death is part of the day-to-day.
Last night, a beer was paid to Mr. X, who died during the night.
This morning it is his son who will finish the beer at the feet of the deceased.
'We help relatives as well as patients,' says Nathalie Savard, director of care.
At the Victor-Gadbois house, the days follow each other but do not resemble each other.
Alongside a 93-year-old man savouring his last family assembly in the racing yard, well locked in pillows while a 36-year-old man dies tragically dying, surrounded by his parents, wives and two young children after cheating everything to survive.
"For the last six months, there are still three to five beds occupied by cancer patients under the age of 45," said Dr Christiane Martel.
5 per 33 per cent of patients who are admitted to Victor-Gadbois come from their home and 47 per cent from the hospital.
Problem of access to palliative care
Seventy-seven 7% of Canadians simply do not have access to palliative care, i.e. treatments designed to soothe suffering when a patient has reached the terminal stage of life, whether at home, in the hospital or in a nursing home.
And several organizations, such as Victor-Gadbois and Greater Montreal Home Palliative Care Society specialize more or less exclusively in the care offered to cancer patients.
It is precisely this great deficiency in Quebec health-care that makes several doctors specializing in palliative care fear the adoption of legislation regulating euthanasia and assisted suicide.
Since the month of October, a Manifested Manifesto, signed by Palliative Care, including Dr Balfour Mount and Dr Bernard Lapointe, is circulating to show their opposition to such an initiative.
According to Dr. Christiane Martel, the Quebec health-care system is not efficient enough to ensure that everyone will be entitled to quality palliative care before anyone agrees to euthanasia.
"Recently," she said, "I've seen a patient spend 14 days in the emergency, in great sorrow, without the necessary steps to relieve her.
I'm afraid that patients only ask to die because they don't receive adequate care.
At the same time, several oncologists are struggling with their patients until the last day, despite the worst prognosis.
Hélène Richard's prospects for survival were already minimal when she gave up stressful chemotherapy.
When I told my oncologist that I was stopping the treatment, she told me that she regretted that I would stop fighting, she said.
Yet she had told me that I was finished.
Not omnipotent care
Dr. Martel believes that 90 per cent of those who ask to die thank the caregivers for not having responded to their request after being relieved of their sorrow by a palliative care team.
But it has to be said that palliative care is not absolutely omnipotent in the treatment of pain.
According to Elsie Monereau, Palliative Care Director at Greater Montreal Home Palliative Care, patients are refractory graduating from pain in 8% of cases.
At the very end of their lives, doctors often resort to palliative seducation, which amounts to putting the patient asleep until the time of his death, either sporadic or permanently.
One can no longer pretence not to hear that part of suffering.
Increasingly, the unfascinated patient will have the opportunity to have this palliative sedation.
Sick patients who are not relieved always say the same sentence: "I want to die."
But that doesn't necessarily mean, "I want you to euthanize me," that means "I want to be relieved."
