The price of bitcoin, after having reached a peak of 30 dollars (23 euros) in June 2011, fell to 2 dollars, five months later, before coming back today at around ten dollars (prices are listed on bitcoincharts.com).
Nothing veryémocrificious, compared to world exchanges in realcurriculum or financial products.
However, the European Central Bank (ECB) took an interest in a report on virtualcurrencies published in October.
It describes bitcoin as 'virtual currency having the most success', "competing with the dollar or the euro" and "similar to conventional currencies".
Bitcoin distinguishes itself from other types of virtual currency such as "characters," used to progress in a video game that one wins by playing or that you can buy (and sometimes redevolves in return).
The social network Facebook has also developed this kind of system.
But each time a central authority monitors and treats trade.
With Bitcoin, all the nodes of the network are depositories of the book of accounts, verifiers, currency issuers, and buyers and sellers.
How does this network work?
Each transaction between two users is actually made between two e-mail addresses like an e-mail.
Except that a user can choose a different address for each payment, thus ensuring anonymity.
A set of information linked to this transaction is signed electronically by a dual-key encryption system.
The network can thus verify the authenticity of the transaction.
By virtue of the contents of the file, it is also possible to ensure that the bitcoins exchanged do indeed exist in the public gaming book, distributed throughout the network.
The key step consists in writing this new transaction in this book.
It goes through the resolution of a mathematical challenge for the computers, and whose winner, a kind of temporary central banker, will have the privilege of adding this extra line.
This is a file hash phase, i.e. transforming a large file into a shorter and unique digital fingerprint.
The computers "prepare" the new transaction and add to it a number before "capping" the whole.
The aim is to find the number which gives a particular imprint (much zeros at the beginning).
Once this number has been found, the other nodes easily verify that it is the right.
The transaction is then indestructibly linked to the chain of all the other transactions; any modification would change the imprint.
If to decelerate, a user wanted to pay twice with the same money very quickly (less than ten minutes), only one of the two transactions would be validated by the network - the other remaining orphan because the two have different fingerprints.
Completing the challenge wins 50 bitcoins.
To avoid inflation, this reward is halved steadily, probably before the end of 2012.
The number of bitcoins in circulation is therefore limited to 21 million, but they are divisible up to one hundred millionth, which leaves some room...
Difficulty of the challenge is also taken into account at each increase in computing power.
The life of the network has already had ups and downs.
Websites providing services for Bitcoin have been attacked and bitcoins in stolen deposition.
"The feavity used doesn't concern the protocol itself," says Pierre Noizat, who has just launched Paymium, a real-currency payment company using the Bitcoin network.
The ECB also mentions the possibilities of money-laundering through this anonymised service.
Cash also has this defect.
Famous actors such as Wikipedia refuse donations of this nature.
Others, such as WordPress blogging platform, accept them.
Recently, Adi Shamir et Dorit Ron of the Weizmann Institute in Israel analyzed the account book and showed that nearly 80 per 80 per cent of bitcoins do not move.
In November, "giant walls" were launched.
'Three Thous Thous Thousand Dollars have been exchanged,' Jon Holmquist revolts, working for Coinabul, which converts bitcoins into gold.
Pierre Noizat, also author of an educational book on this currency, greatly believes in the potential of this technology as a network of transactions.
Its system, Paytunia, is tantamount to a credit card (either-currency) or contactless mobile payment, but it uses Bitcoin to validate transactions, which are cheaper.
The bearer also manages its identity and can therefore be anonymizer.
The system is easy to implement among merchants, who do not need to install new terminals or software.
Simply communicate an address that a telephone can "photograph and recognise", says Pierre Noizat, who claims to have thousands of users.
There is a general movement to challenge hierarchical systems for more horizontal systems.
'It will take time for Bitcoin, but 2013 could be a turning point,' he predicts.
The ECB, in its report, also plans to re-evaluate the various risks, which are now considered to be lenient, in the event of success of this currency.
We went from Afghanistan.
What next?
French troops left their area of responsibility in Afghanistan (Kapisa and Surobi).
NATO and the United States are expected to follow at the end of 2014.
It is the time for the Afghan army regaining possession of its territory and for the Afghans to choose their future, without waiting for us.
Above all, it was the Afghan peasants whom we punished them as terrorists.
And ourselves, with our 88 soldiers killed, and the wounded, mutilated them.
The Taliban are made up of foreign extremists, former refugee rulers in Pakistan, but often peasants who refuse the foreign armed presence, such as in the days of the Soviets.
They want to defend their ancient and archaic traditions, even though they have been joined by jihadists, Pakistani, Arab, Uzbek and Tajik.
Tolerated, sometimes aided by local insurgents, they will no longer be helped when Westerners become more discreet.
The departure of the French troops from Nijrab base, which I observed from the upper hills planted with almond trees thanks to French credits, was done in order.
Concensorship and armoured convoys joined Kabul unimplemented, overflown by helicopters.
There will be no Taliban surround at Kabul at the end of 2014.
Circumstances have changed since their irresistible progress from 1994 to 1996.
Kabul was then empty, the country with fire and bloodshed as a result of the struggles between different factions.
Their takeover of the country at that time was perceived as a kind of liberation, a return to security.
The Afghan women have paid the price for the obscurantism of these peasants overwhelmed by the organization of Al-Qaeda, but their situation has not improved today.
Ancient mujahedin, the current Afghan government and the Taliban joined in the desire to keep women in a inferior position.
The main protruders of the anti-Shustic-Anti-Shushered Warfare returned to power in 2001.
They turned into businessmen, seizing government land and reselling it into building land for returning refugees from Iran and Pakistan, with huge US subterior contracts.
They disrecognised each other; indeed, most of them did not fight themselves.
People, as I have heard in the countryside, yearn for a government that is not thieves.
A lot of young people want to leave, as will those who have been able to take advantage of American largesse will leave: capital flight is considerable.
Young people are tired of war and its ideologies.
They lived with the modern world during their exiles in Iran or in Pakistan, they appreciated the benefits.
Approximately 65 per cent of the population is under the age of 25; Kabul now has a population of 5 million, one fifth of the total population.
In the cities, government schools are full, of both girls and boys.
It will be necessary to provide work to these young people who will no longer want to return to the obscurantism of the former parties or the corruption of certain leaders.
All, including armed opponents, were fond of mobile phones; television and its Turkish soap operas, which presented a modern world, was followed everywhere.
The army is now present.
Were the authorities that would order it considered to be legless?
Elgeous anti-Soviet commanders are already preoccupied with reconstituting provincial militias from central government.
Afghanistan, a country of mountains with strong local identities, should be able to benefit from some decentralization, such as Western nations, but the United States had wanted to transform it into a centralized State, with strong presidential power, removing the post of Prime Minister that had existed since the Constitution of 1964.
President Karzai does not want foreign controls, especially in the elections scheduled for April 2014.
But since the 1950s his country has depended heavily on foreign-aid aid since the 1950s.
No industry has been restarted, no dam is in the state and no major irrigation systems are being repaired.
Everything is imported; nothing is produced except fruit and vegetables.
The priority is left to the private initiative.
In a country ruined by thirty years of war, government control over infrastructure would have been required.
Noise was plentivable that Afghanistan possessed immense mineral wealth.
This only added to the feeling that Westerners were there only to seize it.
Without energy to treat iron or copper ore on the spot, nor means of transport to export it through the mountains, there is no mining.
The Chinese had already almost left the copper mine in Mes Aynak, letting international archaeologists (financed by the World Bank) excavate the huge Buddhist site and remain the most important employers in the province.
There will also one day also require Afghanistan and Pakistan, on which imports and exports rely heavily, replenish normal reports.
Departure of the French fighting troops ended on 20 November.
The new co-cooperation treaty provides for the continuation of traditional forms of aid: lyceum for girls and boys, department of French at the university, French Institute, cooperation in the military, legal, medical and agricultural fields and support to the Archaeological Delegation.
Since 2009, in an attempt to "gain hearts and minds" and to carry out the impossible task of coinciding aid and offensive actions, a "civil-Military actions" service of the Ministry of Defence (Cimic), abolished in 2012, has carried out many works of collective interest and agricultural renovating in dozens of mountain villages.
This labour-intensive work could help to contain the insurgency: irrigation, wells, drinking water, reforestation, fruit-bearing trees, soil protection and an increase in cultivable areas.
What will we leave as a memory, after two billion euros of military spending?
A much more modest budget would contribute to the improvement of local living conditions, which were very difficult in these valleys, often located at an altitude of more than 2 000 metres.
The Embassy received dozens of written requests for small-scale agricultural achievements from local communities in the province of Kapisa.
In order to free themselves from the insurgency waged by foreign groups, which the farmers have told me, it will be necessary to maintain a small, well-checked civil aid on their behalf that directly touches them.
A Forced Constitution in Egypt
This is another poker shot by President Mohammed Morsi.
While Egypt remains more divided than ever around the constitutional declaration, which temporarily gives it full powers, it has decided to play its course.
Taking everyone away, he announced on Wednesday that the Constituent Assembly would vote on its final text the following day.
Just a week ago, the Head of the State allocated an additional two months for this House to complete its work.
For almost two years now, Egypt has relied on an interim text, amended several times, which weakens institutional stability and leads to legal imbroglios.
This new initiative has only widened the gap in the country.
According to his opponents, the President perseveres in his "autocratic delusion", continuing to 'trail his words' and 'feed the law'.
On behalf of its supporters, it is ensured that this is a way to put an end to the institutional and political crisis as soon as possible, by speeding up the transition process.
A referendum should be held within 15 days.
A very short period of time that forces the Brothers to abandon their plans to explain the text, article-by-article, to the Egyptians.
For the President, it is also a way of regaining popular and democratic legitimacy while drifting is raging throughout the country.
Mohammed Morsi seems confident that the Egyptians will vote favorably, as he stated in an interview with the American weekly Time.
All the more in view of this use of a hasty vote stems from an ultimatum to the Egyptian people: 'either you are voting in favour of my text or I keep full powers', which supposedly comes to an end after the adoption of the Constitution.
It was in a weird ambience that 85 members of this Constituent Constituent Constituent Assembly, with a large Islamist majority, voted for the text yesterday.
Most of the Liberals missed the appeal.
In mid-November, shortly before the constitutional declaration, they shattered the door, believing that they were unable to express their views.
The same had been done by representatives of human rights, religious minorities or civil society.
In order to achieve the necessary quorum, 11 of the alternate members were added hastily yesterday in the morning.
Some are very close to the Muslim Brotherhood.
Unsurprisingly, it is usually unanimously that the articles were voted on.
Indeed, commentators have amused himself by the fact that only one of the differences of the day has been expressed around... time for pedagogment, as some members of the committee believe that the pendulum of the Constituent Constituent was ill-resolved.
The text, which was still in the process of being voted on yesterday evening, contains 234 articles.
Articles of all attention, article 2 finally remains the same as that of the 1971 Constitution, stipulating that "the principles of sharia are the main source of the law."
Salafist parties, for which the establishment of an Islamic law was a major claim, hoped to be able to replace "principles" with "the rules", which would have permitted stricter enforcement.
For the Islamists, the fact that this article has not been changed is a token of their good will and their respect for the other components of Egyptian society.
"Hypocrissis," replied the Liberals, who saw it only as a flash of communication.
According to them, the Islamization of the Constitution is done through other articles.
These include article 220, which gives Al-Azhar University an advisory role, in particular with regard to verifying the conformity of the laws with sharia law.
According to Egyptian expert Sophie Pommier, this is worrisome because 'People called to vote are not elected and have no democratic legitimization.
You can see the beginnings of an theocracy."
The fears of the liberals are also fuelled by the fact that the next rector of this university should be much less moderate than the present.
"For the time being, there is no concrete religious involvement.
With this Constitution, we remain within the framework of the Civil Registry.
Most of the jurists who have worked on this text are not ulemas but academics, for some of them trained in the tempered French Alexis Blouet system, who is preparing a dissertation on the Egyptian constitutional transition.
However, it recognizes that "there may be an ambiguity around article 220, because the terms used borrow from religious vocabulary.
Reference is made to “fiqh”, inter alia, [Islamic Juris Prudence.
In the future, the question could arise as to the extent to which civilian judges are competent to rule on them."
In addition to its religious aspect, the text adopted yesterday is strongly criticized because of the extensive powers it granted to the President of the Republic.
The Muslim Brotherhood argues that it is significantly reduced compared to what they were under the former regime.
Another contentious point is the powers conferred on the army.
In accordance with the wishes of the military, consideration of the defence budget will not be submitted to Parliament, but to a National Defence Council.
Nor will the trials of civilians in military courts be banned, as requested by human rights associations.
They also expressed their concerns about the text, which they derelied to be liberticidal.
Blasphemy offence was maintained and insult was now prohibited, which could have a serious impact on freedom of expression, particularly in the press.
In addition, there is no longer an exception to the protection of women, says Heba Morayef of Human Rights Watch.
In her view, the only positive development was the prohibition of torture in article 36.
The word did not appear in the previous Constitution.
While the Egyptian president was scheduled to speak on television last night, protests are planned this afternoon.
Supporting the Head of the State will be paraded on Saturday.
In Israel, holy places, the Centre of the World and a sea of brine await Ukrainian tourists
The Promised Land unifies great biblical truths, modern comfort and wilderness.
Argoumenty i Fakty chose five main reasons for travelling to Israel.
Leave to worship holy places
Unmissable rendezvous: the shores of the Jordan, where Jesus was baptized.
It is said that whoever penetrates these "baptismal funds" will be touched by divine grace.
As for Galilee, it is the land of Jesus' miracles: where the water was changed to wine, where Jesus walked over the water, where he appeased the torvigor or where pedagogment was miraculous.
It was in this specific place that Jesus appeared before his disciples after the Resurrection.
But where you will find the most holy places, it's good in Jerusalem.
Believers browse through the Street of Suffer or the Way of the Cross (Latin: the Via Dolorosa).
This path begins near the fortress Antonia (the praetory), where the trial was held, then led by the streets of the Old City to the Church of the Holy Sepulchre on the Golgotha, to the place of the Crucifixion, then to the Stone of Anointing and Tormbal Stone.
It is here that the Christian center of the world, a symbol of the salvation of humanity, is located.
According to Christian legend, the monastery of the Cross was erected at the place where the tree used to make the cross on which Jesus was crucified.
Jerusalem is also the hilliest place of the Jews: the Lamentation Wall which constitutes the rest of a Temple destroyed by the Romans in 70 BC.
According to the tradition, people of different denominations leave small words with prayers that are then answered.
Drive in height
Visit the ruins of Fortress Masada, an ancient refuge against enemies built by Herod the Great in 25 BC for his family.
These ruins lie on the steep rocks of a hose 450 m above sea level.
Only mountaineering enthusiasts can visit it on their own.
For the others, a cable car takes them to this summit full of history.
In the north of the country, between 1,600 and 2,040 m above sea level, is the Mount Hermont Ski Ski Ski Ski Ski Ski Restaurant Ski Ski Resort Mount Hermont Mount Hermont Mount Hermont Mount Hermont Mount Hermont Mount Hermont S
A shuttle bus can take you from the foot of the mountain.
Ski slopes have a total length of 45 km.
Old crees want pagan gods once indulging the top of the mountain.
Visit unique museums
This country is home to nearly 300 museums.
Unnecessary to visit them all during a single stay.
Here are the five of the most interesting that are really worth the detour.
First of all: The Israel Museum located near the Knesset (parliament).
It houses the ancient manuscripts of Qumrân, or manuscripts of the Dead Sea, discovered in the Caves of the Desert of Judée, as well as nearly 500 mile of archaeological and anthropological artefacts.
You can also be seen: Tel Aviv Museum of Art.
It mainly exhibits the canvases of Impressionists and expressionists Monet, Pissarro, Renoir, Cézanne, Sisley, Matisse, Modigliani, Chagall and Picasso.
In Acre, Hammam Al-Basha awaits you with its Turkish baths composed of moulds from visitors and overseers of the time.
Once in Césarée, enter the only Private Ralli Museum where you can admire sculptures by Rodin.
There are no guides or souvenir shops.
Advertising is free of charge and donations are prohibited.
The fifth museum is Yad Vashem Holocaust Memorial, located in Tel Aviv, retrieving the most dramitting historical pages in history.
The most tragitibilication part is the Children's Memorial Build in Memory of the 1.5 million extermined children in concentration camps and gas chambers.
Thou wilt enter it and pass into total obscurity.
Stars glitter.
You then hear the names of the Jewish children and the names of the countries in which they died.
Ukraine is also mentioned here.
Health and well-being
The three major beach resorts of Israel are located on the shores of the Mediterranean Sea, the Red Sea and the Dead.
Each of them offers swimming pools, aquatic parks, dolphinariums and giant aquariums.
It should be noted: you can bathe in the Red Sea even in the winter, because the temperature of the water does not fall below 21, while the temperature of the air may be up to 23 degrees Celsius.
The Dead Sea is even warmer and you can bathe there all year.
By the way, it is the most unique sea in the world: it occupies the lowest point of the Earth, 417 m below sea level.
Azur-color water contains brine and very easily retains bathers, even those who do not know how to swim.
The landscapes that surround it are surreal.
We come to carry out a treatment based on salt water, silt, curative bulb and to cure various diseases such as dermatitis, allergies, asthma, eczema, arthritis, bronchitis, diabetes but also to simply relax.
Discover the Secrets of Antiquity
They are guarded by the Old City of Tel Aviv, Jaffa, on the shore of the Mediterranean Sea.
It is routed by the famous Via Maris which connects Egypt, Syria, Anatolia and Mesopotamia.
The city is mentioned in Greek and Egyptian legend of antiquity.
According to legend, it is here that Noah built his ark and Persia saved the beautiful Andromeda, with whom he lived a long and happily life.
Tourists particularly like to stroll through narrow alleyways that bear the names of the zodiac signs.
It is said that if you touch the walls of the street corresponding to "your", fate shudder you will shudder.
In Jaffa you can see newlyweds who have come to take a photo shoot of everything from all of Israel and even from other countries.
In Caesarea, the town of King Herod, you can stroll through the Roman theatre, "conquer" the Fortress of the Crusaders.
Under the Roman Empire, Caesarea was the main city of Judea and the residence of the Roman procurators, including Pontius Pilate.
Carefully restored theater is now used for evening concerts and opera performances.
Ranking for tourists
If you go to Israel, don't worry about your English: about 30 per cent of the population says Russian.
For your trip, it is better to prefer dollars to the euro, because they are easily traducable in shekels (today 1 dollar is equivalent to 3.8 shekels).
As far as urban transport is concerned, these are mainly buses, but Jerusalem is equipped with a high-speed tram and Haifa owns the only subway line in the country, composed of six stations and capable of connecting the lower and upper parts of the city.
It is almost an underground funicular.
The journey in all types of public transport costs 6 shekels and allows you to make changes for 1:30.
In Israel, the Jewish tradition wants to celebrate the Sabbath on Saturdays.
From Friday evening to sunset on Saturdays, markets, shops and public transport energise activity.
And the working week begins on Sunday morning.
Many cafes, restaurants and hotels serve only kosher food, which excludes pork, seafood, non-scale fish and dishes composed of both milk and meat.
It offers a multitude of dishes based on lamb and  beef, soups and desserts based on coconut milk but also hummus, traditional Jewish dough, different sauces, moulded chickpea balls, falafels, fruits and vegetables.
There are no stray dogs in the streets of Israeli cities.
On the other hand, there are a lot of well-nourished cats that roam huddled air.
You can see them in the evening sleeping on the roofs of parked cars.
These felines like passers-by places and don't refuse to rejoice them.
Leasing of a car varies depending on the brand from EUR 37 for 24h (Hyundai Getz) to EUR 188 (Audi A6, Volvo S80).
Insurance costs EUR 15 per day.
Renting a bike will cost you 15 shekels during the day.
Entrance to the museum costs an average of 30 shekels.
The language of numbers
Three million tourists from all over the world visited Israel in 2012.
These were mainly people who came to travel and rest from the United States, Russia, France, Germany, Italy, England and Ukraine.
118.8 miles of Ukrainian tourists visited the Promised Land from January to October 2012, an increase of 51 per cent than in 2010, prior to the rescisation of visas on 9 February 2011.
'While language in Moscow goes': migrants save money to learn the language
While MPs and human rights activists pollustrated on what the mandatory language test law is going to give, fraudsters selling "false" certificates have already appeared in the country
Every year, 13 million migrant workers come to Moscow, St. Petersburg and other Russian cities.
Utilities are nationals of Central Immazonuities: Uzbekistan, Tajikistan and Turkmenistan.
Their only goal is to make money to help their families staying at home.
As of 1 December, a law had entered into force and stipulated that every migrant worker would have to undergo an examination of Russian.
For the time being, this rule concerns only those who intend to work in services, housing, personal services and retail.
However, over the time, the Federal Migratory Service promises, the tests will become mandatory for all immigrants.
In addition, it will be necessary to validate the knowledge of the language, but also that of the history of Russia and the foundations of the rule of law.
It will be necessary to validate one's language proficiency in Russian in order to obtain and renew one's work permit.
Only nationals of countries where Russian has the status of an official language are exempt.
Persons who had obtained educational certificates or diplomas before the fall of the USSR in 1991 are also not covered by this law.
Beneficiency, negative attitude and protection of rights
The network of examination centres will be under the auspices of the Pushkin Russian Language Institute, the Peoples'Friendly University, Moscow State University, St. Petersburg State University and other higher education institutions in Russia.
Immigrants will be able to take the examinations in all the cities of the country: more than 160 centres have already been opened throughout the country.
This initiative to introduce reviews was supported by the deputies of the State Duma and the Federal Migration Service.
But before the law comes into force, human rights defenders have already repeatedly put the question in the press: what is it going to mean?
What the obligation to know Russian will change for Russians and Immigants?
First of all, the representatives of the migration service reply will make it possible to reduce the number of people suffering from "slater slavery to work".
Many talk about defending the rights of migrant workers, explains the director of the Russian Federal Migration Service in Tajikistan, Victor Sebelev.
The protection of rights must begin before their departing.
Only an organized selection system will make it possible to solve 90% of the problems of foreign workers.
The problems arise for migrants without professions, without training, those who do not know the language and do not have medical certificates.
Without an understanding of language, Sebelev certifies, the future migrator is destined to meet dishonest people who, under the pretext of helping it, will provide him with a "travel" in an overcrowded cottage where him and dozens of his peers will torment for months without food and without paper and will have to trim between 12 and 14 hours a day.
We receive numerous complaints from our migrants.
"In their homes, we promise them one thing, and when they arrive, we deceive them, we take their passports, and they don't receive the planned salary," confirms the Head of the Directorate of Migration for Labour of the Tajik Migration Service, Tolib Charipov.
Don't bother me, policeman.
However, many nationals of the Central Asian republics who wish to come and make money to Russia say that they do not only know the language of the country to which they want to go, but also that they write with difficulty in their own language.
Of course, this is not their fault, but their misfortune: few Turkmen, Uzbeks, Tajiks can afford even fair primary education.
Indeed, their families don't even have enough to feed their children, let alone dress them properly and equip them for the school.
As soon as they reach adolescence, these young people start earning money as soon as the opportunity presents itself.
It's difficult when you don't know the language, they confide.
"you feel diminished and diminished."
Human rights defenders note, however, an important aspect of the Language Act.
The examinations will be intended solely for migrants who have a legal status.
Without status, there will be no formal review or work thereafter.
Yet most migrant workers continue to live illegally in Russia.
"Welcome or defence of entry"
A lot of Immigrants explain that it isn't so easy to obtain official status in Russia.
The reason? Administrative Barriers and Always These Language Difficulties.
In addition, legalisation costs money: between 12 and 16 rubles.
And then we can get falsic recording quickly for just 1,500 rubles.
The Russian police know that we have falsifying documents, that we don't have a record, resulting in extortion.
'They are asking us for 100, 200 rubles to buy cigarettes and tea,' said journalists Umed Khushkadamov, a citizen of Tajikistan.
"Keep, don't skimp and buy yourself a beautiful piece of art"
From the first day of entry into force of the Language Act, it had been discovered that migrant registration documents were not the only ones to be untruded.
False language examination certificates had already been confiscated by employees of the Federal Migration Service.
Falsified papers come out of an ordinary color printer.
Of course, their new landlords did not get them for nothing: every migrant who thus hoped to make it easier for the examinations had to spend seven thousand rubles for this document.
It is two and a half times more expensive than the official procedure: it will cost the migrant worker three thousand rubles.
Government representatives and human rights defenders agree that the short-term objective is to protect the system against corruption so that it is impossible simply to buy these 'paper streams'.
The authorities could give migrant workers who had not been able to validate the examination on the first occasion for attending language courses.
In addition, Immigrants who do not know Russian will be offered work in areas that do not involve actively communicating with people.
The Ministry of the Ministry of the Interior doesn't deal with the arms market
The proportion of crimes involving legal weapons is very low
The Russian Ministry of the Ministry of the Interior proposes to energize the law against civilian carriers.
This is the reaction of the authorities to recent incidents: whether the shooting at weddings or the massacre organized by the Moscow jurist Dmitry Vinogradov, who killed seven people.
The police wanted to prohibit the wearing of rubber bullet weapons in public places and raise the legal age for obtaining a weapons licence from 18 to 21.
The idea was supported by the head of the Duma Committee for Security and Anti-Corruption, Irina Yarovaya, who promised that the amendments to the Arms Act would be introduced in the State Duma in the near future.
Not everyone is happy that the Russian authorities are trying to solve the problem by 'clutching the Sceve'.
An open-ended letter has appeared on the Net, in which its authors, representatives of various firing centres, are demanding to give up “this senseless hardening”.
The percentage of crimes committed with a recorded weapon is minimal, the BBC jurist and crime specialist Vasiliy Lesnikov told the Russian department of Vasiliy Lesnikov.
According to statistics of the Ministry of the Interior, over a six-month period in 2012, 142 crimes were committed with a firearm registered in the police, while 1.18,18,000 crimes were recorded over the same period.
Perpetrators of the open letter are certain that the strengthening of civilian weapons laws will not prevent criminals from garbating the black market.
According to them, any weapon can now be found at a reasonable price in Moscow.
However, the Ministry of the Interior asserts that it controls the dissemination of illegal weapons.
Suppliers: from factory to officer
The arms black market is supplied through several channels.
There are five main canals, says Victor Baranets, a retired colonel who served ten years in the Ministry of Education and the Quarter-General.
Screen-screenshot from a site taking command of weapons
The first is "Military Trace", i.e. weapons that were stolen during military operations in the Caucasus.
'These weapons were stoked by Russian officers, but also by the Caucasians,' explains Baranets.
The second type of weapons from the black market are those stolen by criminal law enforcement officials.
Baranets explained that it was both weapons stolen from police stocks and those stolen directly from law enforcement officials.
Lillegal pistols go for sale from military warehouses.
Many of them have often experienced thrilled burglaries.
'Registered versions indicate that certain fires were deliberately triggered to conceal the deficit,' explains the former soldier.
For Baranets, weapons manufacturers have something to do.
'Private arms factories that do not compete with competition on the international market and sell arms on the black market, especially in Moscow,' says an expert.
One of the other sources of the black market is smuggling.
A large proportion of pistols and subobeyers come from poor countries, such as Kyrgyzstan.
'It has a local, sometimes artisanal production, a Mafia has formed and organises the supply,' adds the former soldier.
Where do the weapons come from?
The experts assessed the approximate share of each source of illegal weapons on the black market.
A report on this issue was established in 2011 by the Public Administration Problem Analysis and Study Centre.
Specialists analysed reports from the Ministry of the Interior and Rosstat, the criminal records and the public data of the weapons-related gates.
The vast majority of illegal weapons, the researchers explain, come from the army and law enforcement agencies.
Half of the weapons on the black market found themselves "for the lack of employees whose work is linked to weapons," the report explains.
According to the researchers, 17% of the weapons come from military conflicts, 14% are stolen at the time of manufacture and 5% come from 'sclanderous slopes'.
A salesman and consultant to an armoury who asked to remain anonymity assures that for a long time no one buys the weapons found by these clandestine search specialists; they are too old.
In his view, in most cases, sellers would look for new goods in military warehouses.
The person buys a unit, e.g. an TT pistol, through an ensign.
We deliver it to him by passing the goods through a mesh.
"He transports it to the city and resells it for EUR 900 per unit with two loaders," he recounts.
