Lately, fans of new tech have become accustomed to perceiving augmented reality through special glasses.
Initially, Google unveiled its current model this summer at its an annual conference, and in November it was learned that Microsoft applied for the patent.
However, it transpires from the interview with the director of the Interactive 3D Technology Group at the Microsoft Laboratory, Shahram Izadi, that for the company's scientists, the glasses are already from ancient history.
They are tempted by the prospect of manipulating virtual objects in the naked-to-armed air, through the creation of open virtual spaces.
- Could you tell us in a few words what your research group is doing?
- At the same time, we are working on the interaction between humans and the machine, and at the same time we are trying to broaden the boundaries of this interaction.
For the time being, humanity as a whole has stayed at flat-screen pixels and sometimes tactile pixels.
We want to look 5 to 10 years ahead of us and guess the radical changes that will be taking place with this interaction.
For for example, the Xbox console and Kinect sensors constitute a step forward, and almost no Xbox sells today without Kinect because everyone is interested in gesture control.
– What does the future in store for us as well?
- Although Kinect has rendered physical interaction, a lot of things happen on flat-screwing, sometimes in 3D.
Entrieving the information has been improved (the system receives more data), but the performance, for the time being, not really.
We're trying to change that, and we're working on truly 3D imaging systems running on different technologies, including projection technologies.
We have to bring the computing world out of ours, in the physical world, to make it more tactile.
To do this, however, it is necessary to identify not only the user, but also the space that surrogates it.
In this way, we can complement the real world with virtual objects in a more practical form.
First of all, let's get rid of these stupid virtual reality headsets.
- What do you think about voice control?
It is appreciated, but Isn't it overestimated?
We cannot say that this is a panacea: the question of confidentiality arises because we do not always want to inform the others about our actions or intentions.
Actually, every type of interaction with the computer is good, but each in its niche.
For for example, to order aircraft in public places, we had a project for which we thought of gestures, not broad gestures, but rather discreet, restricted gestures.
The gestures were not captured by the camera of the device, but by a bracelet worn by hand which recorded the movements of the bones and muscles.
It is currently large, but it is theoretically possible to reduce it to the size of a clock.
In fact, the future will be marked by the mixed command, e.g.
- Is that to say?
- For example, how would you ask me to pass this can?
You would do so with words and gestures.
- Usually, I'm just talking.
- Oh, it's going to be hard to recognize.
- So, you want to force users to adapt according to what the machine may or may not do at any given time?
- Not necessarily, but this is a mutual rapprochement.
I believe that in the very near future we will have to work on the development of new types of sensors which will enable us to better identify human reactions.
It can be, for example, laser sensors, they produce a pretty good resolution in depth, and that's very important.
- If we are interested in your work on Xbox Kinect sensors, what don't you like in modern cameras?
They lack resolution, depth or anything else?
- In principle, it's about the current generation that we base ourselves in our work on 3D recognition.
It would of course be good to obtain about 8 megapixels at a speed of 1000 c/s.
But the problem is not megapixels themselves, but matrix quality and depth.
Compared to this last point, all current technologies are pretty good, it adds work to algorithm designers.
It must be borne in mind the resolution along Axis X and Y, but also .
Quotation also matters a great deal, the number of frames per second.
Human movements are quite dynamic, and 30 c/s are really not enough, especially for gestures.
Steven Bathiche of our Redmond lab gave the touch of sensors a regular processing delay of between 1 and 100 degrees, with current sensors being closer to the second value (60-100).
Not everyone understands how this influences the interaction of man and the machine.
I would very much need a device of this type in my work, but needing no touch, to have more frames per second.
"But Shouldn't we increase the number of cameras?"
The Kinect now possesses three “cameras”, one of which is in fact an infrared emitter and the second, a receiver of the reflected signal.
And the third camera is in fact a usual visible field sensor.
It does not serve to define the depth of the object.
A lot of cameras could solve this problem.
Or worsen it by increasing the required calculation volume.
It would be interesting to create a Flexible Kinect-like product, play with the camera curve and see how this could help define locations.
- As far as I can't remember, Microsoft, unlike Google, didn't introduce its glasses to the audience.
Don't you think that from a point of view of the day-to-day use of Augmented Reality technologies, glasses are one of the platforms with the greatest prospects?
- Of course, walking all the time with a Smart Smart Hands isn't practical, but this is what I think: the best option would be "transitory" augmented reality that would allow you from a cloud-to-Smartphone to switch from glasses to smartphone.
Spectacles are a very personal appliance, and that's what's the strength (personal elements are the ones you're the only ones to see) and at the same time their weakness: augmented reality from glasses won't allow you to work on virtual objects in collaboration with other people.
- Anamagine for a moment that the manipulation of virtual holographic artifacts in the air is no longer accessible only to Tony Stark in Iron Man but also to mere mortal.
There is a problem on which the opponents of this idea point their fingers: there is no tactile sensation.
Hands don't feel.
What answers is your group preparing in relation to this challenge?
- In my classes, I often say that augmented reality is the seventh breakthrough in human-machine interaction.
I think the eighth could very well be the addition of tactile sensation.
For the time being, one of the interesting techniques is the use of second-hand to serve as an image support.
It records the pressures perfectly.
But there are also technologies that are really intended to attribute palpability to these "images in the air": e.g. the interference of several ultrasonic rays directed to a precise point where the finger is located and that gives you the feeling, for the moment still weak, of an air breeze on the tips of your fingers.
There are also bracelets worn on the wrinkles which influence the nerve endings of the fingers, they are also an axis of work presenting perspectives.
"And you've been cheating on the brain?"
Making him believe that he feels what he should normally feel when he sees something?
- That's a good idea, we haven't tried yet.
There's still a task down there that it's going to be done for a long time to a long time: the way to make someone who physically finds himself in a very small space that he walks in an almost infinite open space - we are working on the concepts of treadmills (nothing to do with those found in fitness clubs), mobile platforms and also giant balls.
For the time to come, there's only a mean way of cheating on the brain, there are still many years of work.
This is what makes the work on virtual reality so interesting for the researcher: a lot is still at an absolute state of fertilization.
