Giving in space
The Earth is in danger.
Global warming or collapse with a lethal asteroid.
Space shuttle caravans with on-floors on board set out in search of a relief planet.
In order to salvage humanity, a big question arises: how do we continue to sustain our species in weightlessness or on a relief planet?
I don't think there's much choice.
There are, in fact, two real planets that we can conquer, if only by hypothetism.
'This is Venus and Mars,' says Vladimir Sodine, researcher of the State Astronomical Institute P.K. Chternberg (GACIh).
If on Mars the conditions are more suitable for life, the surface temperature of Venus is 500 .
We can only live there at very high altitudes or on the orbit of Venus... in space.
Consideration has been given to addressing the issues of reproduction in outer space, focusing on flora.
Half-year-old experiments were carried out on plants.
Forty-four generations of peas cultivated in an in-orbit did not differ in any way from earth varieties.
And then we orbited insects, small reproductive drosophilic flies.
In 1979, quail eggs were sent into space to see how an epistrous embryo developed.
A completely normal chick is obtained at the outlet.
It is then that the problems begin.
"The problem is because the chick has to find a support, it has to put on its paws and start to move," the director of the lab of the Institute of Medico-biological Problems of the Russian Academy of Sciences (IMBP) Vladimir Sychev.
By finding no support, the chicks agitated in a disordered manner.
And after 10 o'clock, newborns saw their instincts completely atrophy.
Chicks reacted neither to the light nor to sound.
Sadly, they would simply dilate at the end of the fourth day.
"We looked up for them twice, then we gave up, because it's simply impossible to work with them there," explains Vladimir Sychev, noting the failure of experience with chicks in outer space.
The last biological miniature "march" with animals was sent to orbit 16 years ago.
The experiments will continue in the spring of 2013.
However, the bio-satellite "Bion" will only embed species of the same-sex species.
There was an experience with rats sent with their fetus into space.
In principle, nothing supernature has been discovered.
'It was still bio-satellites and then it's the only experiment. You have to carry out this kind of research,' notes Vladimir Sychev.
After landing, the rats in space gave birth to their progeny.
Nevertheless, it is not yet possible to perform reproductions directly in space.
It's no mean feat.
By losing their usual environment, the animals are no longer able to achieve their sexual instinct.
Unlike beasts, man is able, in principle.
Homo sapiens possesses abstract thinking, it is capable of creating a suitable emotional background.
This type of experiment is not carried out for ethical reasons.
But women have been in outer space for 50 years.
Biggest risk was taken by Tereshkova.
And the feminine organism is what humanity is most valuable.
Our seagull flew and nobody on the Earth could tell whether it would hold after a flight into space.
If it could have given birth to a child after the robbery.
“No one answered this question,” explains Vakhtang Vatchnadzé, retired from the rocket and space industry.
In June 1964, just one year after her flight into outer space, the first cosmonaut woman Valentina Terechkova gave birth to a little girl.
The child's father, Andrian Nikolayev, was also a cosmonaut.
In 1988, the second female cosmonaut Svetlana Savitskaya, after having been twice in orbit and even working in an extra-vehicular environment, gave birth to a yarn.
However, the risk persists.
We have little, even very few cosmonauts who have had nothing and who give birth to healthy progeny after long robberies.
"And if they are longer, it presents a danger even during in-street flights," concludes pilot and cosmonaut, hero of the Soviet Union and Russian hero Valeriy Poliakov.
Yet humanity must seek new solutions in biotechnology, radiation protection and the creation of artificial gravitation.
