The head of ISC (Supregrant Institution for Public-Furgment Control) said: 'Chief control of pseudo-self-confidous work ('Technical 'zvarc system') has failed because of politicians.
In the area of public procurement, the Czech Republic has competent inspectors and high-quality legislation, but lags behind its application.
Miloslav Kala, Vice-President of the Higher Institution for Public Financial Control, said in an interview for Aktu-ln.cz.
"The law will never be perfect, but its application must be fair and, in my opinion, this is what is lacking in us," Kala said in his commentary on the current situation.
Joint monitoring carried out by Czech and German inspectors also suggests similar conclusions.
As an example of abusive practice, it cites the approach of the "zvarc system" by Petr Neaas as an example of abusive practice.
The Prime Minister has recently said that ODS does not inconveniate contractors with controls; is this permitted or prohibited?
“The law must indeed be enforced in one way or another, so if it forbids something, even the Head of Government cannot prevent an authority from going inspecting to demand compliance with the law,” Kala notes.
At the meeting of the Monitoring Committee in the Chamber of Deputies, you mentioned a Czech-German project in the context of comparing the public procurement legislation of these two countries.
What was a specific question about?
This is a parallel check that we launched about two years ago.
Simply put, European legislation prescribes how to manage public procurement and specific national legislation, as well as its own practice.
We have put all this together and although this monitoring procedure has not yet been completed, very interesting differences have seemed to us: generally speaking, our legislation is perhaps 'sterni more stringent and of better quality', nevertheless, according to certain parameters, the practice as such is better in Germany.
This therefore confirms that it is not sufficient to continually draw up increasingly detailed rules, but that it is necessary to focus on the very implementation of the law.
How can this project actually help you and what happens to you?
Joint monitoring of this kind should just contribute to our insistence on trying to clarify the laws, to lowering and improving the thresholds, when this does not have a really positive effect.
The principle of good management means obtaining the matter at a reasonable price (which does not always mean the lowest), in order to exclude any enrichment and possible criminal activity.
But by lowering the thresholds of the markets, it will not necessarily achieve this objective.
This system may be so subjugated by red tape that those looking for its facsiwer will find many more opportunities than if the threshold had remained higher.
You are talking about internal problems relating to the practical implementation of public procurement legislation.
How does the monitoring system in Germany work?
Were there an institution an analogous to our SAI or a different mode of organization?
As far as institutions are concerned, similar to the SAI, the Bundesrerechnungshof, which is organised in the same way, it also has a council set up a little differently, but these two institutions proceed basically in a similar way .
Competences are, to a certain extent, to some extent, however, compared to our system: since Germany is a federal structure, it therefore also has courses in accounts of this type at the level of the various federal states.
The BRH can only control federal money, which in our case corresponds to the resources of the state.
The public funds managed at our home by the regions and the municipalities are controlled there at the level of the Lender's auditors.
As far as national legislation is concerned, is it simpler than ours?
I wouldn't want to make a general comparison without concrete facts, however, in some areas, Germany is often referred to as a model, but it certainly cannot be said that it does better in all areas.
Maybe it's due to the fact that they have better inspectors?
Surely not, but rehearsed, I don't have any comparison data.
However, it must also be said that this country, which we see as a model, is also faced with a whole range of problems.
Otherwise they would have no advantage in cooperating with our institution, wouldn't they?
With regard to the national legislation, which has meant to you, as an institution, the amendment of the Public Procurement Act and its effects are already being felt?
Since this amendment entered into force relatively recently, it does not yet have an impact on our monitoring operations.
As we carry out ex-post checks, we have to deal with a certain degree of delay.
Our comments only concern, for the time being, the process of preparing for future controls - we have launched a new 'disqualification of mismanaging risks' with which we have scrutinised almost 14 000 public contracts - these changes will of course be felt, because the thresholds have been modified as well as the conditions of certain types of tendering procedures and so on.
Do you therefore regard the adoption of this law rather as an advantage or an additional burden for the administration?
I believe that this law is a step in the right direction and I hope that the future will confirm it.
The problem that may arise is that these menstruations become tougher and it is not possible to comply with them.
Already under the previous regulations, the provider (e.g. the regional council, in the case of regional operational programmes) forced the controlled subjects to accept that any violation of the Markets Act meant a breach of budgetary discipline.
So is it really useful to make the law tougher in this way?
I don't, I don't.
The system must exclude those who want to attack the system or abuse it, but not punish those who make formal mistakes regardless of the decision-making process.
Such a system will exert more pressure on the administration.
So what is the solution to get out?
Let's see where we don't go.
Recently, the Prime Minister stated that the SSO would not announate the contractors with checks on the so-called 'varc system - what does that mean?
Was it prohibited or permitted by zvarc?
The law must indeed be applied in one way or another, then if it prohibits something, even the Head of Government cannot prevent an authority to inspect and demand compliance with the law.
He can say, 'We're going to change the law and allow it,' but he can't say that we're going to act as if nothing happened.
The Contracts Act contains relatively strict rules on the formalities to be carried out, precisely in order to protect the tender.
But it is also tragic that a bidder is eliminated solely because of formal flaws.
The law will never be perfect, but its application must be fair and, in my in my opinion, what is lacking in us.
