Ornest said: 'In concert, we want to garner positive energy.'
What do you think of less than a month it should be the end of the world?
It's just one of these alarming news, the kind of pitfalls that we like to fall into.
In our group The Tap Tap, we're laughing instead and we're thinking that we're the only group that's capable of garnishing an amount of positive energy to regrow or prevent the end of the world.
In addition, in December you mount an entirely unique project: three concerts against the End of the World.
Can you introduce this project in more detail to our readers?
It is a charity action that we have been preparing for two years now.
We decided to use the marketing potential of the end of the Maya calendar announced for 21 December at 11 a.m.
The previous day, on 20 December, three concerts will take place simultaneously in Prague, Brno and Ostrava.
They will almost come to an end by the Maya Calendar on the Isle of Kiribati in the Pacific Ocean on a 12-hour time zone ahead of us.
Who got this idea?
At the beginning I think it was my idea, then we designed everything with our creator, Honza Augusta.
Apart from the fact that we want to collect enough positive energy to save the world, we also want, together with the public, to reflect on the state of the world that we leave to our children.
At the end of the Maya Calendar, we created a collection of unique artefacts, including shoes, T-shirts, sacks, and Original Keys against the End of the World, by buying them online at www.e-tap.cz people can also support us.
The band The Tap Tap with other performers also recorded an anthem against the end of the world titled "Unrupted End of the Unobstructed World".
It's already very popular on YouTube, will we also hear it at these charitating concerts?
Of course, at the very end, finally, if we succeed and the end of the world doesn't take place ...
It will be sung simultaneously by all performers in the three concerts.
The anthems will also meet at some point in a unique live broadcast of Czech television.
The ly lyrics of this song were also written by Toma Hanik who also played the main role of Jesus in the zip, "Xindl X".
How did you come to cooperate with them?
We are collaborating with other personalities of the Czech biz, due to the fact that we organise numerous concerts and charity events...
We are trying to involve them intensively in these projects.
It turns out that most of them are interested in such a cooperation and that it amuses them.
What are the benefits of these concerts against the End of the World?
To equip the Studeo training centre which is accessible to people with reduced mobility and which we are working to carry out within the framework of the Jedliika Institute, together with the Tap association for six years.
Teachers regularly come to the students of the Jedli-k-v institute and offer activities of interest and amputative to them.
Students themselves don't have the means to go to classes, we're trying to help them in this way.
As part of the completion of the construction of the Jedlyika Institute, we will move this project to a new independent building.
Several music groups and performers will go on stage at each of these concerts?
According to what criteria, do you choose them?
We have tried to develop a programme for all generations, including children.
For, for example, in Prague, will compete on the stage Chinaski, Support Lesbians, Illustratosphere with Dan Boerta, The Tap Tap Tap, Marion Bango and Jic Such.
You'll find all the full details on www.kpks.cz.
Are you also preparing for other "mega-events" of that kind in the future?
In May, for the first time in the spring of Prague; here, too, we will surely be preparing a high-quality programme with interesting guests.
We would also like to perform next year in the House of the Czech Republic in New York, and personally, once on the United States in the United States, I would like to combine concerts in D.C.
Your international projects are minimalist, so far you've played, e.g. in Madrid, Brussels, London and Moscow.
The Tap Tap is nevertheless a group composed of people with disabilities.
How do you manage the organization and logistics of your tours?
It doesn't be as terrl that it sounds at first.
We have five members in an electric wheelchair whose vehicles have to travel in the hold, of course we also have a lot of luggage and instrument boxes...
Until now, everything has gone more or less well, and British Airways were always perfectly prepared for us, they astonished me more than once.
Even in Moscow, where we came from recently, everything happened without any problems.
Thanks to your travels abroad, you can compare how these countries differ in terms of accessibility facilities for people with reduced mobility, the attitude of public opinion towards people with disabilities and so on.
What are your experiences until now?
After Madrid, Luxembourg, London and other places where everything works better than at home, we have witnessed precisely in Moscow that it all is still only in an initial phase.
Compared to Prague, Moscow is still very unavailable to persons with disabilities; there it is not yet the fluctuate for a person to travel alone in an electric wheelchair in the centre of the city.
They do not yet see it as a matter of giving priority to a person in a wheelchair in an elevator.
Happily there too, an association trying to draw attention to the problems of people with disabilities is being established.
Why do we still have to catch up with the more developed countries?
There's a lot of things that we're still late for.
It is important to mention that improvements and refinements always depend on the efforts made by those involved.
In London and Madrid, there is a lot of practice for people with severe disabilities to travel alone in public areas; they can go to the toilets, to the museum, anywhere...
It is still not very common there that a large number of people with disabilities are actively participating in social life, on this point, you, The Tap Tap Tap, are on the contrary at the peak of progress.
Respect for the public is one thing, but the situation will really start to change when we have fancy athletes, performers, actors, politicians or famous jurists.
For the time being, these are only isolated cases of people with a strong will.
The Tap Tap group is currently very popular, let's go back a few years, what prompted you in 1998 to create this group?
I started working as an educator at the Jedliika Institute, where I found myself surrounded by a lot of young people willing to invest in something.
And since I'm a musician myself (I play, among other things, saxophone), with a coworker, we created a music activity.
And then, over time, as our moderator La Angelovic says, we lost a little bit of control of the situation (Laughter).
Success has happened only in the last few years if I am not mistaken?
It's true, connecting with known singers and working actively to promote the group has helped us.
We have understood that if a job is not visible, it is as if it did not exist in the substance.
Thanks to grants from the European Union, we can, in addition, allow ourselves to have high-quality teachers, equipment, and so on.
Did you aim to achieve such a level with The Tap Tap?
From the very beginning, I felt that there was potential to make a difference.
Show business is a lot of things that copy each other.
In a way, it makes sense: everything that's new is accepted with caution and it lasts for a long time.
Few of the Original Projects, I dare say that Tap Tap is one of them.
The first impression of a person who sees us for the first time is naturally pitiful, it's natural ...
But pity is totally useless, because people with a disability aren't suffering, left behind by all, whom we have to pity.
They are people who can live a life in its own right and blossom, provided they have the necessary means, of course.
I often say that when people with a disability manage to achieve something, it is not just a step forward for them but for society as a whole.
Isn't your success also due to the fact that you're a demanding leader, as you're qualified by a lot of people?
If we want to do high-quality work, we must be intransigent in many areas and require a certain degree of discipline.
But I don't think that's normal.
Some people come to our house with a romantic vision and head in the cloches and, when they realize that they have to come to rehearsals twice a week, follow the preparatory classes and spend a lot of time on the roads for the concerts, they quickly lose their enthusiasm.
But it works in the same way in every group that wants to work and achieve goals.
The Tap Tap Tap currently has 20 members.
How many of them were already here in 1998?
Only one, L'a Angelovic.
We're an open group, people come in and leave, we can't do anything.
Our gates are always open to those who want or want to rejoin us.
This will be the case on the ulterior day before the scheduled end of the world, Thursday 20 December 2012 from 9 a.m.
This action will take place in Prague in the Incheba Arena, Brno in Fléda and Ostrava in Plynojem with the participation of twelve groups and other musicians from the Czech Republic.
At the end of the evening, these three cities will be connected by an Audio-Audio-Located Refresher Rescisive Rescisive Song of The Tap Tap.
The aim of the concert is to garrify financial resources (to the tune of CK 25 million) to equip the Studeo Multifunctional Training Centre located in Prague at the Jedliika Institute.
The price of a ticket at this concert is CK 400, admission is free for children up to the age of 12, ticket pre-sale in Bohemiaticket.
Poland and the Cosmo.
Last week, at the European Space Agency's ministerial meeting, Poland was admitted as a twentieth member of the European Space Agency; it is the second Member State in the former Eastern Bloc (the Czech Republic became a full member on 12 November 2008).
Poland had initiated close collaboration with ESA in 1994 and over the following years participated in a number of the agency's projects.
However, the route from Poland to space began much earlier.
Already before the Second World War, passionate Poles were working on spaceflights; they didn't always have an comprehension.
I would like to raise, for example, the conference at the Astronomical Observatory in Warsaw on 6 December 1933, during which he presented ideas from his avant-garde book entitled "Introduction to Astronautics".
The development of this young engineer (born in 1905) left cold listeners, and years later Oternfeld remembered that only Dr. Jan Gadomski was interested in his work.
In 1934, zternfeld was awarded the Robert Esnault-Pelterie and André Louis Hirsch prize for his book "Inplunting in Astronautics".
Said Doctor Jan Gadomski (1899–1966) later became a great propagator of astronomy and astronautics.
Hundreds of articles published in Polish newspapers and wrote many books on this scientific discipline.
Gadomski became a propagator of astronautics of world-famous astronautics, his contribution was notably rewarded by the fact that its name was donated to one crater on the hidden face of the Moon.
As early as 1925 a raisin was built in Poland to be equipped with a rocket engine.
Unfortunately, neither the author nor the details of this project are known.
It is also not clear whether the rocket was used to propelate or curb the raisin.
The only known information about this draisine is that of the press at the time.
Beginning in 1933, Polish artillery began devolving on rockets.
Investigated research was carried out in collaboration with Professors Mieczyslaw Wolfke and Gustaw Mokrzyckiego.
It emerges from the documents retained that this research had reached the test stage.
Of course, with the arrival of the German armies, the search was halted.
In 1937, plans for a photoelectric hunting rocket, drawn up by engineer Rohoizski, appeared in the specialized press and the following year the book "Rakieta - torpeda powietrzna i rakietobomba lotnicza" by an Author named Leliwy-Krzywoblocki was published.
The purpose of these two projects was for the military use of rocket engines.
Just prior to the war, all plans for the use of rocket technology were carried out by Tymczasowy Komitet Doradczo-Naukowy), which coordinated all work.
This committee had been established in 1937, but after a two-year operation, the beginning of the war put an end to its activities.
After the war, further aeronautical work was published in the Polish press thanks to the Polish astronautic Society (Polskie Towarzystwo Astronautyczne).
For the first time, there is talk of a company in the November 1954 issue of the journal Problem, in which there are four more detailed articles on aeronautics.
In one of these articles, written by Prof. M. Subotowicze, it is proposed to create an astronautics society
By that time, artificial satellite projects had already been launched and it was clear that the field of space research was in the future.
At the beginning of 1956, the Polish Astronautical Society (PTA) made efforts to join the International Astronautical Federation (created in 1951) and as early as the Autumn PTA became a regular member.
In the following year, PTA's First President, Kazimierz (1902–1959), became Vice-President of the International Astronautical Federation.
He retained his function until his death in 1959.
Beginning in 1956, PTA made a significant contribution to the successful development of the meteora meteorological rocket (Rakieta Meteorologiczna), which became the first Polish rocket to carry out scientific research.
The first model, RM-1, was completed in 1957 and the first launch took place on October 10, 1958.
This 1800-metre carrier rocket had a length of 80 centimetres and weighed just less than 5 kg.
An improved RM-1A version was subsequently constructed and, in the summer of 1959, RM-2 two-storey rocket flight tests began in Bledov desert.
The Rocket ranged from a length of 1.4 metres and probably weighed 11.5 kg.
The next prototype should have been able to carry out concrete scientific work - the RM-34 rocket should have had a range of up to 14.5 km and used to observe the wind at altitude.
But in 1963, all development work was halted.
The Meteor-1 rocket developed during the 1962–1965 period, succeeded the first RM-type fuses.
This two-storey rocket had a total length of 510 centimetres and an initial weight of 32.5 kg.
Three models (called Meteor-1A, -1B and -1C) were developed; they were distinguished by the dimensions of space reserved for scientific tools.
The Meteor-1A rocket had space with a volume of 0.4 litres, while in Meteor-1B this volume was 0.34 liter and in Meteor-1C 0.62-litre.
The maximum range of all these types of equipment was 37 km.
In the period from 1965–1968, Meteor-2 rockets were developed in the aeronautical institute, the first flight tests began in October 1970.
The Meteor-2 rocket had an initial weight of 380 kg and was capable of transporting a payload of 10 kg at an altitude of about 60 km.
The Meteor-2H and Meteor-3 versions were subsequently constructed.
Mention should also be made of the entry of Poland into COSPAR (Committee for Space Research) in 1960 and the establishment of a COSPAR National Committee two years later.
As part of the Interkosmos programme, Poland was also involved in the space exploration of man-made Soviet satellites, and in 1978 pilot Miroslaw Hermaszewski became the second non-sovitic cosmonaut of this programme after Vladimur Remek.
Repeating the Public Utilities Act is not a solution.
Last week, the Constitutional Court revoked the Public Utility Act.
This decision gave rise to serious debate in the public opinion.
Undoubtedly, it is worth examining this issue from a broader point of view.
Economic systems oriented towards liberalism, both in the EU and in the context of globalisation, are based on the principle of more unregulated economic competition.
In practical terms, this means that the various economic entities as well as national economic systems are perpetually colliding with each other.
This results from the principle of freedom of trade and free (undeemory) movement of private capital combined with unbridled financial speculation.
Considerable differences in the price of work (wages) generate a strain on prices.
The following meaning should be brought to an end: a producer tries through the importation of low-supplied goods to withstand economic competition by "municipating" the price of the competitor, with the aim of removing a greater share of the market and thus increasing its own profits.
On a large-scale scale, this means for most entrepreneurs: either transferring production abroad or buying at low prices or disappearing; the result is high unemployment in countries where labour prices are high compared to other economies.
Given that private capital has no social Responsibilities and thus responsibility for the unemployment it generates, the social security contributions of the State necessarily increase.
This whole situation intensifies the perfect unwillingness of the entrepreneurs to pay taxes and thus to compensate for the economic and social damage caused to society as a whole as a result of this race for profit.
This situation is of such well-known knowledge that there is no need to provide concrete statistical data.
Unscrupulous practices of private capital create a situation in different economies in which the governments of different countries are forced to engage in mutual competition in order to artificially reduce the social level of their own populations with the aim of attracting foreign capital.
In other words, governments put their own people at the mercy of private capital without taking into account the social collapse.
This is manifested mainly in the amendments of the laws in force.
The aim is to economically force its own people to accept the diktat of private capital prices, particularly in the field of wages.
This occurs, on the one hand, by a system of economic constraints in the event of long-term unemployment and, on the other, by restricting employees' rights in the field of labour legislation.
As a result, there is increasing poverty and deepening the differences between the rich and the poor.
In the Federal Republic of the Federal Republic of Germany, there have for years been the distribution of public food for the poor who are unable to support themselves with their own work.
Milliions are already numbered.
In the context of an increase in the competitiveness of the German economy, it is becoming entirely appropriate for employees to receive such wages which the State is forced to pay a supplement in order to reach the subsistence minimum.
For example, such a scandal has been uncovered in the case of employees working as auxiliaries in the Federal Parliament.
Saving measures by almost all the Member States in the southern part of the EU undoubtedly generate a similar situation in which, under the pressure of a catastrophic collapse of society, people are forced either to emigrate (as was the case in the 19th century) or to live with miserable wages, on the fringes of society, in the hope that one day private capital will invest in their country.
We must ask ourselves the following question here: where will this capital come from?
If it comes from other countries of the EU, then poverty will pass from one country to another, nor may it come, or a Chinese, an Indian, an Indian, a Brazilian, a Turk, a Moroccan, an Egyptian or an African will continue to work for a fraction of a European salary.
This also applies throughout Latin America.
The liberal theory and the mass media claim that the state cannot invest in capital in its own economy, that the controlled economy leads to an economic catastrophe.
Private capital strongly supports the idea that the state cannot intervene in the economy through regulatory interventions.
It must then be wondered whether private capital does not influence and, indeed, does not today, according to its own selfish interests, politics and by the same, the State as a whole.
Undoubtedly the answer to this question.
The proof of this is that there is virtually an all-powerful lobbies present in all Member States.
As a result, a desperate situation manifesting itself in the area of corruption by the granting of mutual benefits in legislation, where everything is reprehensible but uncomprehensible.
In the Federal Republic of Germany, the situation is such that, due to a lack of financial resources, national ministries delegate the preparation of draft laws to law firms closely linked to the industry.
These bills were then adopted in the Bundestag.
Total power does not come from the people, as Western-type constitutions declare, but powerful financial groups serving their own interests.
It is clear that democracies oriented towards liberalism are now rapidly confronted with the situation described by Appianos in his book entitled "The Crisis of the Roman empire in Caesar and Pompey": 'The state has long been decaying and functions were attributed by violence.
Brigibe beatings, illegal fiduces, and stones and sphragils were also used to obtain these posts.
The bribes and corruption prolifered without limits and the people went to the elections with voices already sold"... ... "Honest people didn't seek in any way to obtain functions, the result once the state stayed eight months without consuls"... "We really began to say that the cure for this difficult situation was an autocracy and that you had to choose an energetic man."
In conclusion, as in antiquity, current society is based on the preference of personal profit, regardless of the interest of society as a whole.
Appropriatedly, private capital is no longer capable of understanding and asserting the interests of society as a whole.
As in the past, it results in an unprecesed decadence from elites, without any effort to carry out in-depth reforms.
We must therefore seek the reasons for the emergence of fascist and communist regimes in unscrupulous liberalisation of the economic systems of the 19th and 20th centuries.
As things stand, the demise of these systems can be seen in favour of liberal-style democracies as a kind of pause before a new stage.
The fact that the current elites are completely ignoring the possibility of hundreds of thousands of future loss of life, the humanitarian and social crises of which we are witnessing today and the crimes against humanity as we know them in ancient and recent history.
Repeating the Public Utilities Act is not, at least in the long run.
Under the pressure of economic competition, at both international and internal European level, the Government of the Czech Republic will be obliged to continue to seek ways of reducing the social level of the population.
This trend is indeed systemic.
The solution consisted of socio-political reforms, which would strengthen State capital investment in the economy, increase the influence of citizens on the government and weaken the monopoly position of private capital in society for the benefit of the State.
Israel: Chaos Laboratory. . . .
'Nothing will ever come out of the violence and will never succeed,' said Sting in the song entitled Fragile whose main verse of the chorus is "Let's not forget how frail we are."
"If my sons didn't want wars, there wouldn't be any," said Gutle Schnaper, the wife of Mayer Amschel Rothschild on his death bed in 1849.
As a result of the latest wave of violence between Israel and the Gaza Strip, as usual, there has been many reactions.
Some took the side of Israel, citing its right to self-defense and placing the Palestinians in the role of terrorists, others supported the Palestinians, invoking the racism of the State of Israel, the genocide committed against the Arabs of Palestine and regarding Israel as a terrorist State.
I do not want to reflect on the question of who is culprit and who is a victim, in his waves of recurrent killings; after all, the current inhabitants of Israel, including those of the self-governing territories, were born in the current political situation and did not experience the onset of the violence.
I would like to offer readers a "behind the Curtain" point of view, on the one that mainly enjoys the tensions that have already persisted for more than 95 years (from the Balfour Declaration in 1917) in a small piece of country in the Middle East.
Some of these reflections are based on historical facts available for study, others come from my own comprehension from that, or more precisely from the group of individuals, which is the main instigator of the events of modern history.
The history of humanity is, first and foremost, the story of a struggle for power.
In every era, we can find an Alexander the Great or a Napoleon.
What is not so obvious is whether these individualities acted independently or whether they owed their deeds to the throne to someone who directed their actions in accordance with predefined objectives.
We have to admit that we are living in an epoch when global wealth is concentrated in the hands of a few individuals and that this financial concentration enducing certain powers could not take place over the course of a single generation.
Among families with astronomical richness is particularly distinguished the Rothschild family, which could be regarded as octopus (I don't know if these families still have a leader, but I don't exclude it).
Little is written about them.
