The movie Argo: When it's all the worse, call Hollywood.
In November 1979, a mob of protesters composed of Islamist students stormed the U.S. Embassy in Tehran and held 52 diplomats hostage.
They had to be released in exchange for the Shah, Mohammad Reza Pahlavi, who after the Revolutionary had fled to the United States, which had supported his regime for several decades.
The situation did not offer the United States administration of a positive outcome; it could not, in fact, abandon the Shah, because it seriously undermined the confidence of its allies in other countries.
However, in Iran, where the revolution had resulted in the establishment of an theocratic regime, it was not in a position to secure the release of the hostages.
It was a severe blow to the prestige of the United States, which was further aggravated later by the fiasco of the attempt to release the hostages by force.
Sequested diplomats were finally released 444 days later, following negotiations organized by the Algerian government.
In the United States, their lyric of solidarity and an anti-Iranian sentiment in the United States.
The Iranian debacle strongly helped Jimmy Carter’s defeat against Ronald Reagan in the 1980 presidential election.
The film Argo, by the actor and director Ben Affleck, describes an an episode in this story that offered a small victory to America.
Immediately before it was occupied, six employees escaped from the embassy.
After complicated adventitious adventures, they ended up in the residence of the Canadian ambassador.
In collaboration with the Canadian authorities, the CIA managed to get them out of Iran with a shocking blanket: they left the country with Canadian passports as members of a filming team that came to spot for the shoots of a great science-fiction film.
Combination of gender
The exfiltration expert plan Tony Mendez required Hollywood collaboration.
In order to make the legend more credible, specialist magazines reported on this film project, press conferences were organized and a fictitious production company had real offices.
The details of the operation remained unscitible for a long time, the film being based on Tony Mendez's memorabilia.
Affleck film is an unhealthy combination of several genres.
On the one hand, a realistically designed political thriller, but it's also a "caper movie", the narrative of a successful stroke -- such as, for example, in Ocean's Eleven Trilogy, Twelve, and Thirteen.
ambiences follow one another: documentary-style Tehran sequences (in sub-titrated sequences are presented iconic photographs from the epoch of events and the manner in which, thanks to these photographs, these same situations have been transcribed in the film, there are no big differences),
Heavier passages of Hollywood, treated with irony and some exaggeration,
and then scenes from the headquarters of the CIA and other institutions with costume men discussing the situation around conference tables, in the hallways and on the phone...
Ben Affleck succeeded in restarting his career in a remarkable way.
From a ridiculized actor, he became a respected director, and his anactor's acting stopped arousing ironic remarks.
Argo was his third feature film after the Dark Scientist "Gone Baby Gone" (2007) and Thriller "The town" (2010).
It is also Affleck's first unparalleled film in his hometown of Boston.
The sense of the atmosphere of a place is one of the features that placed its first films above Hollywood standards.
Affleck also demonstrates this sense of the atmosphere in Argo, where the role of Tehran is "held" by Canada.
The best scenes of the film take place on the streets, during the reconstruction of real facts; the initial sequence of the fall of the embassy is impressive by its clarity which also suggests confusion and unprevisitivity occurring when suddenly, in a given place, a page of history is written.
Affleck and his collaborators achieved an analog effect in fictitious scenes (the falstical film crew in the Tehran Bazaar).
Too much action in too many places
The filmmaker had to content himself with a subject actually featuring few thrilling scenes in the cinematographic sense.
It is doing very well, it sometimes adds certain elements to the reality, and the result is not always as elegant (the scene in which the threat of a crisis at Tehran's airport is sidelined by a phone call in America and the continuation on the airstrip is quite drawn by the hair).
Argo’s weakness lies in its dissipated character, due to the need to show too many events in too many different places.
While it's true that Alan Arkin and John Goodman are quite charming as Hollywood cooperators, their characters deserved a greater place, and they're not, far from there, the only ones in this case.
Affleck's film lacked a bit of dramatic tensileness, with moderate interest, appreciating the realization and retro style of the '70s.
It entrains the spectator, but with difficulty.
As a remembrance of a story still, in some way, unfinished to this day, and as an illustration of the exaggerated nature of the representation of the secret services as omnipotent and omniscient, it does the trick.
Rules for inflating balloons, bananas and circus
The server www.bankovnipoplatky.com, which carries out an annual public inquiry into the most absurd bank charges, has now decided to launch the competition of the settlement or ideas from the most absurd "EU workshop".
'The fact that the EU recently intends to adopt a quota of 40 per cent women for the management of major European companies has been at the origin of our decision,' Patrik Nacher, the publisher of this investigation, Patrik Nacher, told the newspaper.
Among the nonsenses mentioned is, for example, the recent decision of the European Court to unify insurance premiums for men and women.
Until now, however, women had an advantage in terms of life-to-life insurance rates because, objectively, they pose a lower risk to insurance companies.
Until the end of the year, people can report such an unpleasant idea of the 'EU workshop.
"The vote will take place before the end of February 2013", said Nacher.
Controversial EU regulations include, for example, an obligation to add bio-component components to fuels, which has adverse consequences for the environment or a ban on the use of reliable mercury thermometers, only because they contain a relatively small amount of a dangerous substance or the clerk-sizing directive, which has led to an increase in egg prices.
Criticism had also in the past been levelled against the de facto prohibition of the use of the term "butter lapse" or the withdrawal from the sale of conventional bulbs.
High-quality bananas must be 14 centimetre tall.
The European administration is often reacting under the pressure of a commercial or industrial lobbies whose demands are usually defended in Brussels by a state or a group of states (for example, the Czech Republic is currently imposing requests from its banks by threatening to use its right of veto).
The interests of lobbies are also, for example, at the origin of the fact that in the Union, bananas must not be less than 14 cm tall or have an "abnormal curvature".
The European Commission defended itself by arguing that it was only polishing non-homogeneous national standards that complicated trade.
Standards relating to fruit and vegetables had been relaxed despite opposition from some States, claiming that the existing directives led to a waste of food.
The first prize can be awarded in this investigation to last year's EU regulation that an inflatable balloons must appear on inflatable balloons indicating that children under the age of 8 should not inflate without adult supervision.
The EU has referred to a US investigation that balloons are the leading cause of suffocating child deaths.
A similar restriction applies to children under the age of 14 with a view to blowing into ferry trumpets.
In our house, too, we have weird ideas.
The following rule concerning European civil servants themselves is quite absurd: due to the sensibilities of this issue for Greece, anyone with an official position within the EU cannot use the name "Macedonia" and must, instead, talk about FYROM (Former Yugoslav Republic of Macedonia).
In collaboration with the "Leave Faire" association of liberal economists, the server Bankovn-poplatky.com named in this survey, in addition to certain absurdities mentioned above, the EU regulation on the volume of food reserves in the territory of the Czech Republic.
The EU had set maximum volumes for food reserves that could be found on the territory of the Czech Republic on the date of our accession to the EU.
The Czech Republic then went on, for example, as the volumes fixed for canned mushgoth mushgoats, thereby risking a heavy fine.
The organizers of this survey found it interesting to pay sums of money to certain countries because they had no access to the sea and the idea of granting a subsidy to apply for grants.
However, these ideas come not from Brussels but from Prague.
'Dren't having a sea is duplicating us.
In the autumn of 2004, the Minister of Agriculture Jaroslav Palas (SSD).
It argued that, as a result of our wheat production, and as a result of so-called "interventionist purchases", the State had ended up with full stocks that it then had to export.
According to Palas, the EU should have provided us with millions of euros, because the Czech Republic is away from the ports.
At the end of the day, the European Commission responded positively to the Czech demand and organised a call for tenders for the purchase of wheat from countries without maritime access.
Subsidy grants were offered to the municipalities by the Ministry of Local Development headed by Minister Pavel Nemec (US-DEU), which specifically had to be used to draw up applications for grants granted from Brussels.
The EU says: 'weird regulations are rare'.
While regulations are often criticised in the Member States, according to a large number of experts, the efforts made by the EU in regulating and improving the efficiency of the functioning of the Union as well as in the area of its overall development deserve more praise.
More serious is, according to experts, the problem of the use of EU grants for projects which have absolutely nothing to do with the strengthening of European integration, but which were imposed by the Member States in the budgetary negotiations.
The fact that the Czech Republic, like other EU countries, must fight in Brussels for the right to use specific denominations for these traditional products (fight from which it does not always come out victorious), arouses strong emotions among the Czechs.
If at the end of the six-year battle against the Germans and the Austrians, the Czechs successfully defended the name 'Olomoucké tvarky', in terms of the 'Tuzemko rum' which dates back to the 19th century, our producers have had to replace it with the name 'Tuzemak'.
Only products based on canoe-sugrance alcohol, and not based on beet sugar, may bear the rum denomination.
The Official List of Registered Products in the United States of the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland (White of Labeck or Parma ham) is now accompanied by world-famous cheeses such as Feta or Gorgonzola, the cartoons of Labeck or Parma ham.
Pardubice gypw ("Pardubick pernok") and cake cakes called "Cornets de Hozice" ("hosické trubi-ky") can also be proud of their Community brand.
People want me to save the Republic, but I'm just an enthusiast, says Senator Okamura
Senator, how do we come to stand in the presidential elections?
It doesn't matter to be a senator or president that matters to me.
If everything worked without problems in our country, I wouldn't be a candidate anywhere.
But I can no longer bear to see how the country has looted over the last 20 years that thieves aren't locked up and taxes and the retirement age is rising.
I didn't have the ambition to become a politician.
But when I see something I don't like, then I'm trying to find a way to change it.
And since I am more than forty years of age and an independent, a member of any party, I have no other solutions to influence the situation than standing in presidential or senatorial elections.
You went to the senate, but you quickly set out for the Château
Don't you shoot their backs in this way to those who elected you?
I've always said that I'll start the battle for the Castle according to the results of the seatorial elections
Afterwards, I made it clear that if I was elected a senator, I would run for the presidential elections.
My objective is not the function, this is only an instrument allowing me to realize my vision.
That is why I need the greatest influence and the broadest mandate.
The problem is not only as a nation we moan in the bar or in front of our TV set, but it's also that we're trembling coughing those who are trying to bring about change.
Added to this is the press that breaks people's repertancy and confuses freedom of speech with freedom of lyry.
It has, for example, been said to me that I bribe the journalists or that I was the counsellor of Jic Paroubek.
Let's come from your visions.
You began the struggle for the Castle with as a battle horse the themes of the material and criminal responsibility of politicians and retroactive tax declarations of more than EUR 20 million.
To that end, you have to change the law.
As a president, you don't have such power, and only the Senate as a whole can propose laws.
How do you want to solve this problem?
When, as a citizen, I lobbied for tourism guide activities to be a liberal profession, I won.
The problem is politics: when someone arrives from the left or from the right with a good project, it is knowingly rejected and it delays the citizens.
As an independent, member of any party, I am much more likely to gain the support of all the parliamentary parties.
I have the advantage of being able, without categorization and partisan dogmage, taking from each party the best for our country and putting it into practice.
Do you rather consider yourself to be a man on the right or on the left?
From a Czech point of view, I have the impression that people are rather on the left.
For me, whether it's a little to the left or to the right, it doesn't really matter.
For me, the most important thing is for us to move forward.
What matters to me isn't that people are on the left or on the right, it doesn't matter to people.
I always support any solution contributing to the common good, even if it is proposed by the communicist Party or ODS, and I act equally impartially against bad ideas.
Are you enraged when someone tells you that you're a pulp?
But doesn't what you've said prove?
When developing a business plan in a company, you also have a certain optimal goal and vision.
You're trying to get closer.
Some may call it populism, but all the proposals that I am talking about have been implemented by the world, and well-recognized experts have talked about it.
But without the support of the parliament, you only have slogans left.
You won't survive in politics for a long time.
Do you think that if you're going to see people and talk to them, you'll succeed, for example, in imposing material and criminal responsibility?
I don't have another option.
We must convince politicians, journalists and public opinion and try to rally them to my camp in order to impose it.
If I were elected president, it would not be a matter of retransmitting directly on television my request to the presidents of the parliamentary parties to legislate on the material and criminal responsibility of politicians, civil servants, judges and public prosecutors.
Then they should possibly explain why they don't want them.
Once there is a strong personality who identifies the abuses, it will be possible to put pressure on the political stage.
Take, for example, the election of the president by direct suffrage; it was obtained thanks to the pressure of public opinion.
Convey, bluntly, being an enthusiast, not an genius or an intellectual.
I'm trying to find allies for my opinions and visions.
I've just gone into politics and I'm trying to get the support of the majority for my program.
Alone, I'm trying to move things forward, and if it doesn't work, I'll stop in six years and go back into the private sector.
It's like Okamura should save the Czech Republic.
I'mn't a savior.
I know that a single person cannot make a difference, which is why I went to a number of personalities to ask them to run in the senatorial elections as well.
I met Radim Janura, but he refused due to the amount of work.
At the same time, I supported the Investigating Journalist Jana Lorencova, who exclaimed the bunker fraud.
I introduced myself because people are really dissatisfied, but now I'm hesitating.
Sixty per cent of people did not vote, and for the most part they gave their vote to a representative of the establishment.
In the Senate, there are only two independents.
People have elected a snake that can hardly impose any change.
In spite of everything, I will fight for my vision, for example the direct election of mayors and prefets from the regions.
Are you contagring to found your own party?
No, not yet, because I don't have the time to check whether the past of each potential member is unblemished and then I don't have the financial means.
I don't have any money for my presidential campaign either, I only have twenty thousand in my 'transparent' account.
You don't have any money?
You're talking about tax returns, what's yours?
I estimate the value of my personal property at about 60 million.
In Prague I have land worth EUR 25 million, an apartment with a value of ten million, an apartment worth EUR 8 million, a collection of works of art with a value of about ten million, an Aston Martin worth EUR 3.5 million and an zkoda Superb with a value of EUR 1 million and some millions in my account.
By the same to the way, I own an Aston Martin, because it was a dreagan: I've always loved James Bond, who drove, was gallating with women and fighting against evil and abuse.
You ride an Aston Martin, you have an estate worth 60 million and you don't have any money for your campaign?
Do you want to change the Republic but you don't want to invest your own money?
It's unconvincing.
I don't have 15 million for this campaign.
Should I get into debt?
I've already invested two and a half million in the countryside.
The fact that I don't have any sponsors shows that there's no great deal of interest in my program.
I don't be obligated to pay this campaign out of my pocket.
My campaign expenses are generally covered by the remuneration that I will receive as a senator.
But I wouldn't be able to make a living out of it, such as paying my son's English school, which costs 30 thousand a month.
If my only goal was to make money, I won't come to an election.
So you're going to continue your commercial activities in order to be able to live?
Didn't you say you'd muzzle your activities?
But this is not related to the amount of this remuneration.
As I promised, I partially reduced my activities.
For example, my deputy will take the direction of the travel agency in the spring.
People want me to be a samaritan who saves the Republic.
However, I have to live with something.
What was your usual monthly salary when you were an Entrepreneur?
I'm winning and I'm still earning between two hundred and four hundred thousand.
If I were president, I would put an end to my commercial activities.
