The  architects of MVRDV prove that real adventures aren't just about imaginative.
'I think this building is funny, it's very futuristic, finally something interesting to see,' said Lisette Verhaig, a bystander on the side of the street.
Stefan Spermon, an informatician at a big nearby company, says: 'Yes, it's indeed very beautiful, this building.'
But I wonder why we still need a library today.
Everybody has the Internet, an iPad and e-books.
Nowadays, no one goes voluntarily in one of these cheerleading libraries, right?
Spijkenisse, a dormitory-city that has nothing attractive to the gates of Rotterdam, holds a rather particular record.
The municipality of 80,000 has the lowest rate of education in the Netherlands.
In order to restore a few neurons to the brains, the city decided a few years ago to contribute to the general culture and to reproduce the seven fictitious bridges that appear on the euro banknotes in the form of pretty little miniatures made of painted reinforced concrete.
The success of the Cultural Offensive remained limited.
For example, the city authorities realized that there was only one way of regaining control of the statistics: the construction of a library.
Winy Maas, from the Rotterdam architectural firm MVRDV, master of daddy-thresheart diagrams and creator of witty and often cynical edifices, accepted the project with its usual phlegm and took part in the competition in 2003 with five pounds under the arm and sneer on the face.
And while the members of the junction still looked at each other, baffled and shrugging their shoulders, Maas frightened him, stacked up his books of choice in order of size to turn it into a pyramid, and finished his speech backed up by activist arguments by one: "Common Dear."
Here it is, my proposal for Spijkenisse's pound mountain, or as they say, the Boekenberg.
Eighteen years later, EUR 30-bunty-million-dollar hugged.
The edifice is part of a regenerative project that also includes an underground car park, a supermarket, a post office and a few adjoining buildings and houses for a total of 50 dwellings.
In early November, the book mountain received the second "Best Library of NL 2012" award.
In addition, the project was selected for the 2012 Dutch National Wood Award.
For example, this prickly dull habitat, which until the present had nothing more to offer than a post-modern pedestrian area and a mayor of lottery ugly, whose white facades invite to believe that they houses a dairy, has enriched itself with a contemporary architectural work.
The most important is that Spijkenisse now owns the first public cultural edifice in its history.
The Long Road to Placing
Print Image: Eldorado from the book under cheese bell.
Admittedly, an elevator takes you to the heart of the massif, but the real joys of reading and architecture open to you when you tread the floor of the building to discover its topography.
The interior made entirely of glass is illuminous and clear, the Dutch brick floor and elegant streetlights speak unequivocally the language of municipal public squares.
The urban atmosphere is perfect.
We're already looking for public benches, dogs and little boys and little girls playing racing.
And everywhere books, books and more books everywhere.
'Normal, library waists lie along the facade and in the middle there is a large and dark room, which is often uncomfortable and impersonal,' explains Willy Maas.
We completely disrupted the classic spatial configuration and opened the reading spaces from the inside to the exterior.
The interior of the book mountain was skilfully furnished: in the middle are the desks, the Internet library, the chess wagon club, the environmental centre and the building's power plant.
A particular beauty lies in the black rays of the library, which are sometimes glimmers, sometimes balustrades, sometimes staircraps.
A new experience for sight, touch and smell.
Even hardened architects and building engineers don't want uncharted materials.
'We wanted to work with recycled materials,' explains Joop Trouborst, project leader from the municipality of Spijkenisse, in an answer to the Standard.
This is for example, one day we found an ad-negotiated agricultural waste in a Frisian agricultural company.
In the Netherlands, a one millimetre-thrin-thick synthetic diaphragm has been used for years as a substrate in greenhouses and fields.
It's inexpensive and you save time.
Fine diaphragm is used for one or two seasons, and it can be discarded.
For the library, the diaphragm was compressed, for the first time in such quantities, into plates four centimetre-thick.
Under the influence of warmth and pressure, "Landbouwplastic" (KPL) changes its color and turns into a dark, homogenous and resistant material whose odour evokes both a new car and sports shoes.
At the top of the 105 stairs is the top of the mountain.
At the end of almost 500 meters, the literary café offers a stunning view of the city, but also of Dutch croquettes and ficus in jars.
The latter create an atmosphere, but they mainly contribute to the maintenance of the ideal humidity level of literary mountain air humidity.
Gifts for New Soul
'It's unbelievable, but despite all the glass surfaces, this building is a model ecology project,' says Trouborst.
Temperature of the edifice is geothermia.
Although the pound mountain is below a bell of glass, the sun illuminates the interior for a short period of time, even on good weather days.
The thick wooden girders crooked relative to the glass facade serve as stove, intercepting a large proportion of the rays.
The ambient atmosphere is very pleasant.
Fully-accomplated blinds take care of the rest.
Stefan Spermon, the above-mentioned skeptic informologist, has already ventured to enter the new library.
Lisette Verhaig too was already there.
Cynthia Bogarde, a professor of traditional Chinese medicine, even describes the Boekenberg as Spijkenisse's "finally rediscovered soul".
A few weeks ago, at the inauguration, every citizen was invited to donate a book from his personal library.
This measure made it possible to fill the gaps of the library, which had not yet been fully packed (today it has 70,000 pounds).
The concept is a success.
Shelves are overwhelmed from all sides.
Architect Winy Maas, "there's nothing worse than a half-exempt library."
'I believe that our invitation has created a certain relationship between each citizen and this new building.
Everyone knows that his book is part of this edifice.
Even though it's only for decoration."
This is how MVRDV has reached the main discipline that is called, in professional jargon, "the creation of an Identity".
Spijkenisse wrote a new page of literary history.
Even if it may seem young and uneducated.
Finally, it is a new starting point for the birth of an Identity.
Szabo: 'Germans must play a greater role.'
Germany chose abstention during the vote on the admission of the Palestinian State.
Stephen Szabo, an expert on relations between the United States and the United States of the United States, this decision puts Berlin in a delicate political situation.
Deutsche Welle: At the beginning of the week, Germany had announced its intention to vote against the request of the Palestinians to obtain observer state status at the United Nations.
But it then chose abstention.
Why?
Stephen Szabo: Germany doesn't approve of what the Israelis did in Gaza.
However, Berlin must exercise caution, because it has a special relationship with Israel.
But I also do not think it supports the American position either.
Germany wanted to assert its independence, without, however, being too criticizing itself towards Israel.
During the insurgency in Libya in March 2011, Germany also chose abstention in the vote on the establishment of a no-fly zone.
Eventually, NATO imposed this measure.
Is Germany having difficulties in adopting a clear position on major international issues?
The answer is yes.
The reason for this is that Berlin is reviewing its foreign policy, seeking to move away from an American-defined policy and establishing a German foreign policy.
In addition, the lack of a coherent and common policy within the Union complicates the situation.
As a result, the Germans collided with several fronts.
We're expecting them to play a more independent role, but they aren't accustomed.
I think they are still grappling in finding this role, but are on their way to a 'more normal' foreign-politics.
An external policy, such as in France or Great Britain
So what exactly does the 'normal' foreign-policy term mean for the Germans?
It shows the desire to adopt positions independent of those of the United States and European partners, including with regard to national issues.
I believe that German foreign policy is also determined by its economic policy, i.e. by exports and its relations with certain regions, such as Russia, China or the Middle East.
Germany’s economic interests are, to a certain extent, different from those of the other major powers, which is why Germany must protect its interests.
Have these economic interests influenced Germany's position vis-à-vis the conflict in the Middle Middle East and the abstention in the United Nations vote?
First of all, Germany has important opportunities in the Middle East, especially in the Gulf countries.
That is why Berlin must be careful not to offend public opinion, but also the eldest states of the Arab countries.
Undoubtedly, this is a determining factor.
However, I wouldn't give too much attention, because this isn't an exclusively one-sided relationship.
In any case, however, this is a decisive element in German strategic thinking.
Is Germany damaging its relations with the United States by opting for abstention in important votes, as in the case of Palestine?
I believe that Europeans and Americans fully understand the German position.
That is why I believe that the fracture is not as serious as in the case of the Libyan issue.
Perhaps it will even be more respected by Germany.
Indeed, Germany had thus demonstrated that it must be taken seriously as an international actor and that its interests must be taken into account.
In Europe, opinions on the Palestinian initiative are diverse.
On the other hand, the United States announced its veto with a clear vote.
Do opinions differ between the United States and the majority of European countries?
There have always been differences because of the American domestic political scene.
I believe that the Obama administration secretly demonstrates a great deal of comprehension towards the European position.
But in view of the country’s political set-up, the government cannot, of course, defend its position publicly.
I believe that the real differences of opinion are not so great as they always seem.
If you look at relations between Obama and Israeli Prime Minister Netanyahu, you will notice that Obama is a long brim with excitement of Netanyahu’s policy.
Is Germany having difficulties in reconciling its close relations with Israel and the United States, on the one hand, with the position of the major European partners on the other?
I don't think it makes things any easier for Germans.
Of course, it would be a bit simpler for the Germans if there were a coherent and uniform European policy, but now that is not the case.
As a result, they cannot fall within a broader political line and must assert their unique position.
This is what they are currently doing in the issue of the euro.
In my opinion, in the future, Germany will act as a locomotive and push Europe to adopt a European position.
It is not easy for Germany, not least because of relations with Israel.
This issue has always been sensitive.
But in my view, the Germans are well aware of the fact that they need to play a more independent role.
Is Germany seen itself as a major actor in the international arena, does Germany only want to take on a key role?
Or does it still find it difficult to assume leading roles?
Germany is still accustomed, and this is still uncomcomfortable and, for obvious reasons, the country is still having difficulty playing a more important role.
If we look, for example, at the crisis of the euro, we note that whenever Germany chooses to weigh more, many anti-German feelings are heard.
It doesn't make it any easier for the Germans.
In fact, this is always the same problem: we don't want to be encircled by hostile countries.
From this point of view, Germany is in a much more delicate position than the United States.
It must deal with a wide range of neighbours and opinions, and it is not easy.
Today, the influence of the United States on European policy is only diminishing, but at the moment, the EU does not fill this gap, so who is left?
The Germans will simply have to play a more important role.
And even though they don't like it, even if it is unpleasant for them, and if they're less valued, it's life.
Stephen Szabo is the Deputy Director of the Transatlantic Academy in Washington, D.D., an institute in which academics and policy experts from Europe and North America jointly analyse the challenges of the transatlantic community.
Szabo is also a member of the German Marshall Fund, where he specializes in German politics, US foreign policy and transatlantic relations.
"Marseille protection" in China: When Puma and Armani suddenly become Chinese
Armani et Polo Ralph Lauren are well-known brands around the world.
Armani Polo... what's it really all about?
Behind this name lies a trademark officially registered in China that has nothing to do with the original company.
The trade mark thus enjoys such protection that the undertaking which bears its name cannot lodge a complaint.
And even if it could, it wouldn't be certain that it would win.
“It is becoming increasingly difficult for strangers to protect their trademarks in China,” explains Thomas Pattloch, a lawyer from Taylor Wessing for violating property rights in the Far East.
Every week, a new folder arrives at my desk.
imitators only need a few additional letters in order to be able to register their marks.
Thus, instead of Gucci, Lu-Gucci was recorded, and instead of Prada, Prada-Kny was recorded.
German companies are also lawfully copied, like Puma, the manufacturer of sporting goods.
Pattloch opened a fold-and-storey binder containing registrations made with the Beijing Trademark Office.
On September 14, 2010, a Chinese-owned company re-registered DF Puma, a denomination that the firm artificially created by appropriating part of the name of Ermenegildo zegna, the Fashion House.
It's a well-known fact that the Chinese became world champions of copying and violating intellectual property.
In large cities, there are multi-storey shopping malpractices that sell virtually only counterfeits.
Pattloch cases are, however, different: its constituents are responsible for tackling the fact that the Chinese Trademark Office officially gives Chinese companies the right to use a name that is already protected in another country.
The Chinese talk about "Bang Mingpai", a parent brand.
The term is derived from "Bang Dakuan".
It is with this term that wives who marry to wealthy men are designated.
The Chinese authorities say they have nothing to reproach.
"It's bad for business, we have to fight against this trend," Pattloch demands.
"The brand is watered-down, the singularity disappears, the damage to the reputation is enormous."
Financial losses and legal costs amount in millions for the sectors concerned, especially for expensive flagship products.
According to data from CLSA Market Analysing Company CLSA, China represents, with an annual volume of EUR 15 billion, the world's third and fastest-developing market for luxury goods.
However, it is not easy to remove dubious registrations from the trade mark register and these procedures are expensive.
The latter can last for up to nine years, and the outcome is never certain.
Pattloch refers to the trials in which the court dismissed the complaint, as after a long period of time the name criticized had become a "market reality".
Pattloch tells us that if the plaintiff has no luccitrus luck, he may even be obliged to pay money to the plagiarist, because he would have harmed his mark in China.
In this area, the law of the Jungle sometimes dominates.
Famous cases also concern graphic elements.
In 2009, Daimler had lost a legal battle against construction machine builder Sany, the same company that had just taken over concrete pump producer Putzmeister.
This is why the Chinese can use an emblem resembling Mercedes.
Geely, Volvo's acquirer, used a blue-and-bluble logo reminiscent of BMW.
Fashion house Lacoste had lost to plagiates in Hong Kong and Singapore who had redirected the famous crocodile in the opposite direction.
According to the Chinese authorities, they are not guilty of anything.
The CTMO Trademark Office in Beijing admits, however, to have experienced some difficulties in 2010 due to a lack of staff and space.
However, last year the Office reportedly moved from an "emergency situation in the course of the work to a restored normality".
As a result, the number of uninterruptified opposition procedures would have decreased by 22 per cent.
Almost 57,000 such cases would have been closed, an increase of 75 per cent over the previous year.
Nevertheless, 81,500 proceedings remain pending at the Office.
Action is expensive
As usual in China, the figures are impressive.
Over the past year, CTMO received more than 1.4 million trademark applications, almost a third more than in 2010.
Depending on the office, this is a record, and this means that China is at the top of the list for the number of new depots in the world for the tenth in a conjunct.
This would also apply to the total number of legal trade marks of EUR 5.5 million.
In 2011, the Office reportedly accounted for 1.8 billion yuan for registration.
In other words, an average of 1,280 yuan earned an average of EUR 1.280.
A legal battle costs much more, as exemplified by the case of the German family-owned company Freudenberg.
For more than seven years, the company has been battling a Chinese gentleman.
Admittedly, the Germans were able to ensure that the illegal manufacture of vehicle components was suspended.
However, the imitator was able to maintain his Chinese rights to the Freudenberg mark.
"It's our fault, because surnames cannot be protected in Germany," explains Hanno Wentzler, CEO of Freudenberg Chemical Specialities in Munich.
The Trademark Office had therefore rejected the objection of Munichers.
Freudenberg certainly won the case in the next two instances, the opposing party continues to fight until the present day.
Vigi vigilance is required
Today, the case is pending before the Supreme Court.
Wentzler was confident, he believed in a positive outcome of the trial and boasted the professionalism of the judges.
Nevertheless, he adds: 'the procedure is time-consuming, stressful and costs us a lot of time and money.'
It was also difficult to assess internal costs; the Archital Service had even had to review the proceedings of the last hundred years in order to provide evidence.
Five years ago, Freudenberg had even unsuccessfully proposed to the opposing party an 'big six-digit sum in euros' to settle the dispute.
'Tweeting is what it means for us,' says Wentzler.
The dangers of the Far-Orient are even lingering into Europe.
This would be the case if imitators in this region ensured the exploitation of unprotected marks.
For for example, a Chinese producer wanted to name Freudenberg in Germany for a shoe and leather production company.
For a long time, the company had been abandoning this sector, but it could still have prevented the deregistration.
Wentzler adds: 'vigilance is in order'.
Pattloch and advise German companies to exercise great caution in their trade relations with China.
In their view, it was not enough to rely on international trade mark law; rather, foreigners should "recogn also in China everything that could be protected", as Wentzler said.
Otherwise, the costs may be much greater than those of registration.
If Freudenberg lost the battle at the last act of its legal drama, the company would have to pay licence fees to the opposing party for the use of its own name, as Wentzler explains.
Alternatively, we would lose our place in the segment of the market in question.
