At 8.733 kilometers fencing from Heidelberg in the northwestern northwestern of the United Kingdom of Swaziland is Esitjeni, which depends on the vocal power of German choir.
40 per 40 per cent
The village has a population of about 2,000 inhabitants, some live in modest straw huts and the majority of them are children.
More than 300 of them were orphaned, because HIV had taken away their parents.
In Esitjeni, we can observe on a smaller-scale basis the ills afflicting Swaziland: the highest rate of HIV infection and the lowest life expectancy in the world according to UNICEF.
The population hardly ever accepts male circumcision, a measure which nevertheless guarantees a proven reduction in the risk of infection by half.
More than 40 per 40 per 50 per cent of the population of Swaziland is infected with immunodeficiency virus and it is not uncommon to die in their mid-1990s.
During a group trip to Africa in the spring of 2005, the choir had been able to discover the village. The Imbongis mostly saw many children on the street who lacked not only parental care, but also simply everything: food, clothing, education.
In a poor country without a school proficiency, there is simply no perspective.
At the very beginning, it was only a few people who personally undertook to pay a few euros every year in order to be able to send a child to the school and offer him a hot meal every day.
But barely a year later the choir founded “Voices for Africa”, an association that has since been almost professionally concerned with Esitjeni AIDS orphans.
Focimen about Sexually Transmitted Immovable Infection
What are the most common sexually transmitted diseases?
Bacterial STDs include syphilis, chlamying and gonorrhoea (or gonorrhoea).
Common STIs are HIV, human papilloma virus, genital herpes or hepatitis.
Pubis lice and scabies belong to parasitic  STIs.
Which groups are mainly involved?
Syphilis and gonorrhoea often appear in men who have sexual relations with other men.
The Robert-Koch Institute estimates that sexual contact between men causes at least four out of every five reported cases of syphilis in Germany.
In heterosexual adults, psychedeering, trichomoniasis, candidiasis (fungal), gonorrhoea and human papilloma virus are common sexually transmitted diseases.
The rate of spread of HIV between heterosexual adults is relatively low in Germany, but some 20 per 20 per 20 per cent of new HIV infections are observed within this group.
Among young people, there is a significantly higher rate of chlamide-related infections than in other population groups.
According to European surveys, three-quarters of all infections concern young people aged between 15 and 25.
In addition, human papilloma virus in Germany is common among young people.
What is the increase in the number of infections?
It is not compulsory to report all sexually transmitted diseases.
According to the Robert-Koch Institute, syphilis cases have more than doubled in 10 years from 1,967 in 2001 to 3,698 in 2011.
Since 2007, there has been a decline in the number of new HIV infections.
In 2011, there were about 2,700 cases.
This represents a decrease of one tenth compared to the previous year.
What are the symptoms of sexual illness?
Infectious diseases can cause abscesses in the genital organs, urinary pain, white discharge, lower abdomen pain as well as pimples and warts.
However, they do not regularly cause any pain or other symptoms and therefore remain hidden.
How do we protect ourselves?
Condoms may reduce the risk of infection, but they do not protect 100 per cent.
This is because pathogens of sexually transmitted diseases can also be transmitted via lubricant infection or close body contact.
The experts therefore recommend that any person who often changes sexual partners undergo regular examinations.
Most rapidly detected STIs can be healed, and delayed sequelae can be avoided.
