Drink but butter daily, and live up to 168 years
In the south of Azerbaijan, many people reach ages worthy of Patriarchs of the Bible.
It even finds a museum of longevity.
An inquiry into a country, in which we are still relatively young at the age of 97.
In the south of Azerbaijan, many people reach ages worthy of Patriarchs of the Bible.
It even finds a museum of longevity.
An inquiry into a country, in which we are still relatively young at the age of 97.
The trip to the Talysh's mountains is an excavated romanticism.
Looking back at the many turning points of the road, the small bus cahots along wooded hills, torrential rivers and modest farms.
Everything is green and fertile, we would almost believe in the Black Fortnight.
But we find ourselves here in the deep south of Azerbaijan, the border with Iran is only a few kilometers away.
This is where the Caucasian ethnic group called Talysh lives, about whom little is known, except that they speak Persian and Azeri well and have a great longevity.
Terminus: Lerik.
The small plastronist town with its overly aggressive Soviet-periodic architecture that doesn't tune at all with the scenic mountain landscape.
European tourists are rare here, the journey from the capital of Azerbaijan, Baku, is far too painful.
It only takes eight o'clock 323 kilometers, because the road has only one lane.
The province has not yet taken advantage of legendary wealth that the country owes to its oil reserves in the Caspian Sea.
However, Pilata Fatulayeva, aged 48, is convinced that Lerik has the potential to become a tourist location.
'Baku became famous in May thanks to the Eurovision Song Contest, and next last year there will be the Festival of the Older People in the World here,' Fatulayeva explains.
She is the director of Longevity Museum, the world's only museum.
You can discover the lives of about eight dozen Talysh in the region that are more than 100 years old. Fatulayeva shows us a picture in black and white.
This is my grandfather, he reached the age of 120.
Still having a child at the age of 136
However, the controversial star of the museum is zirali zirali, a shepherd who would have been 168 years old.
However, there is no birth certificate.
And when you think the oldest documented person was 122 years old, it looks more than dubious.
"He was born in 1805 in this region he died in 1973," Fatulayeva said.
The man allegedly married three times and had 23 children; by the age of 136, he still conceived a daughter.
Was it a few-deceasing timizing his age for a few decades?
Rembrandt Scholz, genontologue at the Max Planck Institute in Rostock, has also heard of man in the great age of Central Asia.
'surprisingly elderly people also live in some parts of China, in Japan or the Hunza Valley in Pakistan,' explains Scholz, 'in Sardinia too, there are men living incredibly old.'
However, it is not possible to establish scientific evidence due to the non-existence of documents, not to mention that there was no civil status register.
Every day a glass of molten butter
However, it is in fact that people in the Lerik area often reach an age worthy of the patriarchs of the Bible.
Currently, 20 people are more than 100 years of age.
So what is the reason for the longevity in the south of that country?
Farid Mugimzadeh, the Azeri tourist guide, explains that Talysh have particular genes.
On the other hand, Fatulayeva, the headmurder of the museum, believes it is a matter of feeding.
However, from a nutritional point of view, it does not seem plausible that Talysh's high-calorie meals, which love meat, bread and above all dairy products and many of which drank a glass of molten butter on a daily basis, are good for your health.
In Cengemiran, a tiny village close to the town of Lerik, lives Rubaba Mirzayeva.
At the age of 97, it is still relatively young for the region.
Mirzayeva, who claimed to have 143 descendants, lived in a hydo-timbered house, as it was typical to see throughout the Caucasian region.
She sits on the floor next to a churn, the content of which she beats tirelessly.
Eight people here live under a single roof, including one of Mirzayeva's sons and a daughter, who have long been adults.
Two small children also frolic in the house.
Te tea in the kitchen is prepared for guests served in typical belly-shaped armadus lounges.
Mirzayeva White Tooths are perfectly aligned, and beneath his headscarves hide long braid of a dark blond that his son proudly reveals to us.
I've always washed my hair with milk, and that's how I've never lost it and they've kept their color.
"I've never used shampoo," added Mirzayeva.
Monthly rejuvenation is enough
It has always eaten what comes from its own farm: tomatoes, potatoes and peas.
In all my life, I've never bought any food in a supermarket.
Then she tells us about her husband who was in the army.
The worst moments were after the Second World War.
But everything got better when Heydar Aliyev "belly facade" took the orders.
Propaganda language is astonished in an old lady.
However, the cult of the paternal figure of the nation, a man who ruled his country like a dictator, knows almost no limits in Azerbaijan.
He was in power until 2003, his son Ilham later succeeded him.
In any case, the elderly in Azerbaijan did not experience distress.
Mirzayeva receives 230 manats (approximately the same amount as in euros) for his retirement on a monthly basis, a sum that guarantees a cheerful lifestyle in the region.
And maybe Mirzayeva's graying son for a long time is right: 'the eldest have great respect in our culture'.
They are the center of the big family, they are loved, neat and happy.
This is a good reason for living as long as possible.
