Mexican Enrique Pena Nieto had a difficult start.
While incoming President Enrique Pena Nieto was preparing to take up his duties in Mexico, Will Grant of the BBC was interested in the difficulties that lay ahead and the many expectations of the people.
Road traffic in Mexico City is particularly poor at the present time.
In a city where traffic jolts in normal times, a safety dam has been set up since Monday, blocking several key road accesses in the capital and causing real chaos on the roads.
Nevertheless, the aim was not to prevent people from going to work, but to prevent protesters from reaching Parliament.
Next Saturday, the new President of Mexico, Enrique Pena Nieto, will receive the Presidential Scarf to become the new president at the head of the nation.
A complicated task awaits him.
Mexico’s economy is doing well, results for outgoing President Felipe Calderon, but the country is beheaded by a drug war that has already claimed the lives of about 60,000 people in six years.
"My government made a commitment to the Mexican people to reduce the violence," Pena Nieto told U.S. President Barack Obama in the Oval Office earlier this week.
I am going to propose a new security strategy which will enable us to achieve this goal.
Before rubbing shoulders with United States President Pena Nieto as Governor of the State of Mexico.
A populous and sprawling state enclosing the capital. On its predilegive land, opinions regarding this new leader are divided.
"Liquited Man"
In the bucolic commune of Valle del Bravo, for example, it is inscribed in everyone's memory.
The premises attribute to it the development of tourism and infrastructure in the commune.
To reach the city, you can take one of the new motorways of M Pena Nieto, an important improvement has been made to these roads once bursled and full of crevasses.
Patterns featuring his name are hung outside a new sports complex and an inspiring interactive museum dedicated to climate change.
"We are counting on him to achieve lasting change", said his friend and political ally Gabriel Olvera Hernandez, a member of the congress and party of Mr Pena Nieto, the PRI.
Particularly in terms of security and the economy, we look forward to the much-needed real change in our country.
After an unfamous period in power, the PRI was ousted in 2000 by Vicente Fox.
Congressman Olvera concedes that after 12 years outside Los Pinos presidential palace, there are many expectations within the party regarding Enrique Pena Nieto.
It rejects critics of opponents who reproach the new president for running away from charisma.
He is an entire man, very engaged, with an excellent vision of the country.
It is an excellent statesman and, above all, it knows how to listen.
However, this isn't always the image that the people of their former governor have.
At Nezahualcoyotl, also known as Ciudad Neza, the contrast to the cobbled streets of Valle del Bravo could not be more glaring.
Hidden beneath highways, it is, in many ways, a subterranean suburb of Mexico City.
And the problems in the municipality are also concrete and urban.
Earlier in the year, the military had been summonsed to the rescue to combat drug-gangs plaguing the neighbourhoods, where violence against women was particularly serious.
In a stricken area, the bodies of Dozens of women who had been murdered over the past two years had been found at the drizzling plaza.
More than 1,000 women were killed in the state of Mexico while Mr. Pena Nieto was governor, a rate much higher than in the very violent city of Ciudad Juarez - an inshubating place famous for the number of innocent women killed there.
Critics of Mr Pena Nieto say that, at best, he did not manage to stem the problem of the murders perpetrated against women while in office.
At worst, they accuse its administration of refusing to face reality.
In a concrete house, typical of this dilapidated neighborhood, Irinea Buendia wrestles to withhold her tears when she shows me photos of her late daughter, Mariana Luna.
The official version refers to a suicide committed by Mariana in 2010.
However, her family believes that she was killed by her partner.
"When I arrived at her house, it seemed to me that her body had been washed," says Buendia.
There had been signs that it had been beaten, and cadaverical rigidity had already begun.
As her mother tells the story, a picture of Mariana davves the wall, next to a cross featuring the word: Justice.
However, this is exactly what the family was denied.
The authorities treated me as if I were an old commvorcature, a troublemaker, a benevolent.
They want us to accept what they're saying and we keep quiet.
'But it is not right that there should be so many irregularities and omissions,' she said.
When President Pena Nieto receives his scarf on Saturday, it will be accompanied by heavy responsibility.
Dozens of thrifty families had been affected by crime in Mexico over the past six years, and the new president had made the promise that he would give priority during his tenure.
'I hope he'll be as good as president as when he was a governor,' Congressman Olvera told Valle del Bravo.
However, this is exactly what the families of Ciudad Neza victims fear the most.
Bradley Manning didn't complain about ill-treatment, the prosecutors say.
Public prosecutors are trying to counter Bradley Manning’s allegations of ill-treatment during his detention.
The lawsuit concerns the detention of Manning in a military prison in Quantico, Virginia.
The defence wants to close the case on the grounds that Manning was sequestered in extremely difficult circumstances.
The soldier is accused of stealing thousands of confidential documents.
The prosecutors tried to demonstrate on Friday that Private Bradley Manning -- accustomed to being responsible for the biggest leak of confidential information in the history of the U.S. military -- missed many opportunities to complain about the ill-treatment he claimed to have suffered during his military detention.
In cross-examination with Manning, during a preliminary hearing in Ft. Meade, Maryland, Prosecutor Maj. Ashden Fein stated that records of weekly visits made to Manning by officers during his nine-month detention in Quantico, Virginia, did not reveal any complaints about his handling.
Cross-execution -- during a hearing, the defence filed a motion demanding that the Manning case be closed on the grounds that the denaction took place in very difficult conditions and constituted a sufficiently severe punishment -- took place one day after Manning testified to his desire to commit suicide during his detention.
This military intelligence analyst, arrested in June 2010, is accused of stealing thousands of confidential documents during his service in Iraq.
The documents were then published online by WikiLeaks.
WikiLeaks never confirmed that Manning was their source.
At the hearing on Friday, Fein discussed with Manning the forms that the officers had completed after their meeting with Manning during his detention at Quantico Military Prison, where he was subjected to maximum prison isolation from July 2010 to April 2011.
The officers posed questions to Manning and recorded his responses.
When Fein mentioned the forms on Friday, Manning acknowledged that the treatment given to him was "excellent" and that the treatment he had had within the prison was generally "very professional."
The forms did not file any complaints of ill-treatment, although the officers directly questioned him about his prison treatment, Fein said.
Manning replied that he had expressed oral concerns regarding certain problems and that the visiting officers had discussed his concerns and indicated that they would deal with them, but they did not record the issues raised.
'They wrote 'no problem' (after addressing the concerns), but this did not mean that I didn't raise any problems,' said Manning.
Military Judge Col. Denise Lind also asked Manning why he did not complain about his treatment during an interview in January 2011 with the special committee that had met to discuss the suicidal thoughts he had expressed in writing a few months earlier.
Manning replied that its intention during the meeting was to change its restrictive status indicating an "injury risk".
The soldiers stated that they had assigned it this restrictive status -- a level below close surveillance for the risk of suicide -- for its protection and the security of others.
"I wanted the staff to know that I was doing well, and I wanted to be taken away from this status... in order to enjoy a better quality of life from my point of view," said Manning.
Manning testified on Thursday about his arrest in Iraq and his transfer to Kuwait for nearly two months before being relocated to Naval Corps Base Military Prison in Quantico, Virginia in July 2010.
He stated that he had considered committing suicide when he was in Kuwait and fainted once because of the heat.
He added that the fact of not having been kept informed of what was going to happen or what was happening in the rest of the world was very distressing.
"My world was reduced to Camp Arafjon, to this cage," Manning said on Thursday.
I thought I was going to die in that cage.
Once in Quantico, Manning stated that he had spent most of his time in a small cell -- at least 9 p.m. and sometimes more than 11 p.m.
Manning also added that the only items at its disposal were a mattress, a blanket, tongs, clothes and its glasses.
He tried to stay on the move, because sleeping or lingering during the day was contrary to the rules.
Manning also made it clear that when he tried to slept, he had a light in his eyes, lit outside his cell.
If the warriors didn't see his face when it turned around at night, they would awaken it so that they could see its face.
Manning's lawyer filed a formal complaint regarding Manning's treatment in January 2011.
Manning was placed in a military prison in Fort Leavenworth, Kansas, in April 2011.
Also on Friday, the judge asked Manning questions about an allegation reported in his testimony on Thursday -- that after being forced to sleep naked overnight in his cell at Quantico, he was forced to stand naked in front of the guards and other inmates one morning during the appeal.
Manning testified that at no time was he allowed to cover with cover during the call.
During the judge's questions on Friday, Manning stated that he deduced from the order of his guard that he had to let go of his blanket which might have concealed his nakedness, but admitted that no one had ordered him to release it.
Manning testified on Thursday that he had been forced to slept naked the previous night, as he allegedly tried to prove to an officer that he was not a danger to himself.
Manning reportedly told the officer that he could have used the elastic from his underpants or flip-flop to injure himself, but he did not.
That night, Manning testified that his underwear, tongs and goggles had been removed from his cell.
His lawyers hope that at least the judge will take into account his experiences during his detention and reduce his sentence considerably if he were to be found guilty in court martial, a trial that is scheduled to take place early next year.
The defence stated that Manning is contemplating pleading guilty to less serious crimes and fighting for the abandonment of other charges deemed too extreme.
The hearing is expected to resume this weekend, together with prosecutors who should argue that the conditions of detention were justified.
The Pentagon had confirmed that Manning had been incarcerated in accordance with the regulations governing the conditions of detention of all Quantico detainees.
Accomplish charges against Manning include the theft of public goods or documents, collusion with the enemy, resulting in the illegal publication of intelligence on the Internet, and the transmission of national defence-related information.
If all the charges are taken, he is liable to life imprisonment.
My Mexican-American Identity Curriculum
He says that many were forced to leave Mexico because of the lack of
Mexicans tended to blam those who had gone; they reminded the Mexicans about difficult periods, he said.
Navarrette says that Mexican-Americans find themselves caught between two worlds.
During a recent trip to Mexico City, arriving in the lobby, in front of the customs and immigration authorities, I felt lost.
Panels indicated two tracks: one for "Mexicanos" ("Mexicans"), another for "Extranjeros".
I stood motionless for several seconds, not knowing which leeway I was moving.
I grew up in the center of California, where I've been called "Mexican" all my life.
This is an ethnic adjectory, just like the one that my friends in Boston use when they call themselves "Irish", or my friends in New York when they call themselves "Italians".
Afterwards, I became "Memnestium-American."
But here it was Mexico.
And, on the earth that gave birth to my grand-father, there is no need for a special adjective or hyphoto.
I was just an American.
I speak Spaniard, good enough to manage an interview in one or the other language.
But I don't have the vocabulary of an indigenous person, and I can't lose my American accent.
So I picked up my U.S. passport and walked to the "Foreigner" line.
I thought at that point of the week when new Mexican President Enrique Pena Nieto visited President Obama in the White House.
Usually, when the leaders of these two countries meet, their preferred themes are immigration, drugs and trade.
Pena Nieto also wanted to talk about the growth of the Mexican economy, which was one of the reasons why Mexicans stayed in Mexico rather than risking the United States.
It wishes to ally itself with the United States and Canada, and to create a Union for Trade along the lines of the European Union, but in North America.
And Pena Nieto is committed to continuing the war against drug cartels in Mexico, although he did not provide further details.
For Mexico, the relationship with the United States is complicated and filled with brevity.
Most of the Americans probably never considered the fact that in 1848, the United States invaded Mexico and forced its leaders to cede in writing half of its territory at the forefront of the rifle.
But for Mexicans, who are thinking in terms of centuries, and no minutes, memories are everywhere.
Therefore, as soon as a U.S. official criticizes any criticism of Mexico, you begin to hear -- in the Mexican press, and among the elites -- complaints about how the Americans undermine their neighbour's sovereignty.
And Montezuma kids are looking for a fight.
And yet, for Mexico, the most difficult relationship is that the country sustains with the more than 35 million Americans living in the United States.
Do you want us to talk about bitterness?
There are a lot of them.
Mexico had its winners and losers, people for whom the country offered opportunities and others for whom the country did not offer them.
The only reason that exemplifies the large number of Mexican ancestors in cities like Los Angeles, Las Vegas, Phoenix, Denver or San Antonio, is that at some point, in our genealogical tree, a person, maybe a parent or a grandparent, found no opportunity in Mexico and had to go into the north.
And more often than not, this person corresponded to a typical profile -- dark, uneducated spoils from a poor-village, so on.
We are their descendants, and we are loyal to them.
Not Mexico.
And even though we are currently living in the American dream, that we have gone to good schools and that we have good situations, we must never lose sight of the fact that this is the American dream that we are living in, not that of the Mexicans.
Our identity is sometimes disturbed, but our loyalty is self-evident.
We agree with it in the United States.
On the other hand, we know that part of the Mexican elite among the ruling class doesn't like us.
Feeling is reciprocated.
We are reminding them a humiliating defeat, and they tolerate us as if we were inferior individuals and not Mexican enough.
Our Spaniard will never be good enough; our ties with Mexico will never be strong enough.
For them, our existence is synonymous with failure.
If our families had not failed when they lived in Mexico, they wouldn't have left.
And we wouldn't find ourselves stuck on the bargain, living comfortably in the United States, it wouldn't be like lost souls.
My wife who was born in Guadalajara and who immigrated legally to the United States as a child, reminds me that there are frictions between Mexicans and Mexico-Americans, because Mexicans claim their identity more strongly, and the Americans are upset.
Despite being an U.S. citizen of the United States, she felt that she was part of two countries.
On the other hand, a lot of Mexican-Americans that I don't have the impression of being part of one country or another.
We love the music of the Mexican band, Los Tigres del Norte, and Bruce Springsteen.
We have the best of both worlds, but we're not rooted in either of them.
In Mexico, we are regarded as Americans.
And in the United States, we're seen as Mexicans.
And right now, to complicate the relationship a little bit further, as I learned during my stay, some Mexican leaders and part of the intelligentsia want to get closer to the Diaspora.
They want American-Cameroons to work as makeshift ambassadors for Mexico, representing its interests in the United States.
We will have to explain to our American compatriots that Mexico is a splendid country that must definitely visit and put pressure on political leaders to strengthen ties with Mexico.
Yes, of course.
It won't happen.
There is too much bitterness.
And, if we look at wage inequalities, corruption and drug-related violence that are omnipresent, many of us don't believe that this is a fantastic country.
You're going to have to fend for it on your own.
That's normal.
If some Mexicans are not yet prepared to forgive the United States of the manner in which they treated Mexico a century and a half ago, they must therefore accept the fact that some Mexican-Americans always resent them of the way in which members of their families were treated not so long ago.
Hmmm.
"Perhaps we're more "Mexicans" than I thought.
Old quarrels, the new Middle East
The cease-fire between Israel and Hamas is still a feat for peace.
Will there never be a lasting peace between Arabs and Jews in the Middle East?
Another blunshed seems to indicate that such a hope is in vain.
While the same trivial arguments remain about who has begun, dozens of buildings have been reduced to dust; more than 140 Palestinians, most were civilians, and six Israelis were killed; and, for the first time, rockets from Gaza landed near Tel Aviv, the metropolis of Israel and the holy city of Jerusalem.
But while Israelis and the Palestinians seem trapped in their former conflict, all around them, the Middle East is changing.
The Arab Spring shattered everything, whatever we like it or not, the Palestinians and the Israelis are overwhelmed by this regional upheaval.
Perhaps their struggle will escalate even further.
Yet there is every reason to believe that it could help them get out of this lethal impasse.
A war that doesn't make any loser or win-win
At first glance, there seems to be a need for optimism.
Although the cease-wall on 21 November is continuing, this week’s fighting has reinforced supporters of the force on both sides.
The leaders of Hamas, the Islamist movement in Gaza since 2007, boast of forcing the Israelis to give up, even though Gaza suffered a beating.
Having killed some of its leaders and thrifting the 1.71 million inhabitants of Gaza in one of the most miserable and overcrowded corners of the planet, Israel failed to destroy Hamas.
Indeed, Hamas is gaining ground in the West West Bank, the other part of Palestine currently in the hands of Fatah rivals, the other more moderate Palestinian faction.
In addition, the leaders of Hamas might say that time is their ally.
The influence of Islamists in the Arab world is growing stronger, and Hamas has become wealthy and powerful friends.
Turkey, a burgeoning-riding regional allegoal, once Israel’s closest Muslim allegoy, has championed Hamas; like Qatar, one of Golf’s wealthiest and most dynamic states.
Hamas members jubilate and say that the Islamist crescent lurks around Israel from Lebanon in the north, where Hizbullah’s militia is practicing its dominance, passing through Syria, where Islamist-oriented rebels can overthrow Bashar al-Assad, to Jordan, where Hamas allies are threatening the king.
And most importantly, on the southern flank of Israel, the uprising of the Muslim Brotherhood under the chairmanship of Muhammad Morsi in Egypt, by far the most populous and central of the Arab countries, changed the balance of the region.
Hosni Mubarak, the despot that ruled Egypt for 30 years until its fall in 2011, had little time to dedicate to Hamas.
On the other hand, the Muslim Brotherhood are cousins of Hamas, and their leaders make it easier to rely on public opinion.
In future diplomacy, Hamas can emerge as a major athlete that can no longer be excluded even by Israel and America.
In the meantime, proponents of Israel’s hard line will draw the opposite conclusions.
In military terms, Hamas has been plugged into boxes.
The Iron Dome missile  Interrogating System had proved its effectiveness, and many Hamas missiles had been destroyed.
The Israelis will be able to slept on both ears - for some time.
In terms of diplomacy, America is renewing its unfailing support; and many European countries have accused Hamas of being behind the latest wave of violence.
Above all, Israel has prospered, notably under Binyamin Netanyahu, the prime minister, who completely ignored the peace process.
Although rocket attacks from Gaza had killed some 30 Israelis since 2004, Israel had been untouched by suicide bombings, in particular as a result of the severance fenteony in the West West Bank, the main piece of what could be a Palestinian state, which protected the Jewish settlements, which continued to expand despite their illegal status in international law.
Netanyahu, whose Likud party merged with an even bell-and-breaker party, leader Avigdor Lieberman in the run-up to the election on 22 January.
Why pamper these damn Palestinians by giving them a state?
Wouldn't if they operated the West Bank, wouldn't they launch rocket-propelled, as did their compatriots in Gaza?
It is better to keep them behind this wall and hit them as soon as they pull out their heads.
Perhaps hardliners will prevail; but the Arab Spring may make a difference.
Even if Islamists taking power in Egypt and elsewhere displease Israel, their priority will be to tackle the problems in their own countries.
Israel’s defence budget is more important than that of its four Arab neighbors combined.
Starting a war with the local superpower will not help the new Arab governments fix their economies.
Mr. Morsi has worked with Barack Obama to achieve a cease-fire, which augurs well -- and could mark the beginning of something.
Israelis should also think about the future.
With the rest of the Arab world becoming more democratic, depriving the Palestinians of their right to self-departure could create a powder keg that is doomed to exploding one day in the territories occupied by Israel – much like the bus that exploded in Tel Aviv this week.
Repression is already undermining democracy in the Jewish state, and demography is exacerbating this problem as the Arab population increases.
Bloous missions against Gaza every two or three years to strike Hamas will have a diplomatic price.
Both sides need to be pushed by those from the exterior.
The answer remains that advocated by reasonable people on either side, and by most of the outside world and by this newspaper: two states, with a cession of territory by Israel in exchange for security.
A small and short-term hope is that the cease-fire will provide an expedient for those from outside this cause.
Egypt, which must now work to put an end to the circulation of arms to Gaza, like Turkey and Qatar, is in a better position than ever to persuade Hamas to accept the idea of a Jewish state in accordance with the 1967 borders with exchanges of land and shared Jerusalem.
Arabs from outside countries should also put pressure on Hamas and Fatah to unite.
This would contribute much more to the establishment of a Palestinian State than the forthcoming proposal for a pseudo-state in the United Nations.
Mr. Obama also has a vested interest in Israel agreeing to negotiate.
During its first mandate, it did not present its own peace plan.
Returning to the White House, he seemed equally reluctant to establish one.
It is a very narrow vision.
The stability of the Middle East is a vital issue for America.
This requires a peace agreement between Israel and the Palestinians.
Neutral Cigarette Package Laws EnScientrate in Australia
Writings and photographs of diseased organs cover the dark green packets that are all the same, regardless of the brand
This law came into force in Australia is a world first, logos and colours have been replaced by dark olive green and horrendous photos of sick organs and depictions of children and babies whose diseases have been caused by the smoking of their parents.
In addition to the different photos and health warnings, the only difference between the packets, since this Saturday, is the name of the brand, printed in small characters on all packets.
This is the strictest tobacco packaging regime in the world.
The Australian Federal Government has stated that the aim is to deter young people from smoking by suppressing glamorous representation.
Studies have shown that if people have not started smoking before the age of 26, there's a 99% chance that they will never start.
'Even when they are very young, children understand the message conveyed by tobacco manufacturers through their brand image,' said Federal Health Minister Tanya Plibersek, citing studies that have revealed, for example, that the children combined the korune with a logo with a princess.'
Although Australia has the lowest smoking rate in the world and these changes have little impact on the profits of multinationals, other countries are contemplating a similar approach.
The tobacco industry lobbied this law.
Tobacco manufacturers have stated that this would boost the trade in the black market, which will facilitate access to cheaper cigarettes.
'Harvivable consequences will result from this legislation,' said Scott McIntyre of British American Tobacco Australia.
The forgers of China and Indonesia will sell many more counterfeit cigarettes on the streets of Australia.
Others stated that the Act had energized their activities.
A small family-owned company Sandra Ha of zico Import Pty Ltd, has stated that the demand for cigarette casings, such as siencing garrible packaging, has risen sharply two months ago since British American's Imperial Tobacco, Philip's Imperial Tobacco, Philip Morris and Japan Tobacco contested the law and lost in front of the Australian High Court.
Ha stated that zico sold 6,000 to wholesalers and is awaiting inventory renewal.
For us, business is good.
The bountry alone, the experts say, is the popularity of the social media and the population segment targeted by the plan.
After a series of Australian laws banning TV advertisements, sports sponsorship, and the requirement for dealers to hide cigarettes, tobacco marketing is now available online.
Australia has banned web advertising by local companies and websites, but it cannot prevent foreign sites from doing so.
'While you're working in tobacco marketing and you now only have a small showcase to promote your products, the Internet is the prime location,' said Becky Freeman, a public health researcher at the University of Sydney.
Freeman noticed an increase in the contributions of some ordinary citizens to social media brands such as YouTube, Twitter and Facebook.
We have to wonder whether this is a simple citizen who loves Marlboro Cigarettes and has taken the trouble to make a video, or whether this is a marketing campaign orchestrated by an agency?
British American Tobacco Australia has stated that the industry is focusing on the new regulations in force rather than marketing.
Industry went so far as to pay Ukraine, Honduras and the Dominican Republic to challenge these new court rulings - these countries have told the WHO that trade is unfairly disadvantaged, although none of these countries have had significant trade with Australia.
A WTO decision is expected in the course of 2013.
Plibersek stated that the Government had engaged in discussions with other countries regarding similar packaging laws.
Canada was the first country to make warning photos on packages mandatory in 2001.
At the present time, these warnings are present in 40 countries, including Brazil, Turkey and Ukraine.
Stricter laws are currently under consideration in England, New zealand, South Africa and India.
Many Smokers in Australia remain indifferent to these photos.
Pictures don't bother me.
I don't know.
"you take a cigarette and you put the packet," said Victor El Hage at the time of buying a bundle featuring a bone tumor picture.
To be candid, the only reason that will get me arrested is my granddaughter.
James Yu, manager of the "King of the Pack tobacconist" in central Sydney, stated that the uniformity of the packaging made it difficult to stlvedge the packs on the stalls.
"Before, it took me an hour to unload a parcel, now it takes me four o'clock," Yu said.
'The government had only to ban them once and for all, and we would have said OK, it's okay, we're stopping and we're wrinkling under the wreath," he added, hanging out his arms to the heaven.
In a constantly connected world, it can be good to get bored.
I spent five hours at an airport during the Thanksgiving period, because our plane had mechanical problems, and we had to wait for the arrival of another airplane.
As a result, I had enough time to reflect on the issue of boredom.
I won't lie to you.
Spending a half-didnapped half-didnance at an airport is quite boring, even when you have books, magazines, and iPhones to displease you (don't mention duty-free shops).
Increasingly, some academics and experts in child-development are pleasing with boredom.
It is perfectly normal for us - and our children - to get bounced from time to time, they say.
This forces the brain to take interesting paths, or even to nourish creativity.
And since we are, for the most part, constantly facing several types of screens, we no longer know the benefits of boredom.
So should we welcome boredom with open arms?
Yes.
And no.
But I'll come back.
First of all, like a lot of people, I thought that boredom was a rather recent phenomenon, not least since we had more free time.
But this is not the case, says Peter Toohey, Greco-Roman History Professor at the University of Calgary, Canada, and author of "Boredom: A Lively History" (Yale University Press, 2011).
'The boredom goes a long way,' he said.
The walls of Pompeii include Latin graffiti that evoke boredom, dating back into the first century.
Secondly, there is the definition that we give to boredom.
The problem is that it has been defined and discussed in different ways, said John D. Eastwood, Associate Professor of Psychology at York University in Ontario, Canada.
After browsing a wide range of publications and submitting the idea to a discussion group of about 100 people, Professor Eastwood and his colleagues defined boredom as an experience of "waiting to do something, but failing to engage in a satisfying activity."
What differentiates the boredom of apathy, he says, is that the person isn't undertaking a task, although he or she would.
With apathy," he said, "there is no desire to do anything.
What is at the heart of boredom, he said, is "the interruption of the process of attention, combined with a sullen humour and the feeling that time passes slowly."
Boredom can closely resemble depression.
But Professor Eastwood has stated that while the two may be connected, bored people tend to think that the problem comes from the environment or from the world, while depressed people think the problem comes from them.
Sometimes we sometimes think we're bored when it's actually a problem of concentration.
As part of their study, "The Unengaged Mind: Defining Boredom in Terms of Attention", which was published in "Perspectives on Psychological Science" in September, Professor Eastwood and his colleagues highlighted an older experiment in which participants listened on cassette, the voice of someone reading a magazine article.
Some of the bands heard, from the next hall, the excessive sound of an unreported TV show, others just heard the TV show, and the third group didn't hear the soundtrack at all.
Those who heard high-noise TV said they were more bored than the other two bands -- they struggled to concentrate, but didn't know why, and attributed this concentration problem to boredom.
When you're trying to focus on a difficult or engaging task, an interruption of attention can lead to boredom, said Mark J. Fenske, an Associate Professor of Neuroscience at the University of Guelph in Ontario and one of the authors of the study.
On the other hand, when performing a boiling task, "like looking at objects on an editing channel, music can help you don't get boring."
In fact, he says, we now know that when tweeting or scribbling, these gestures, often regarded as signs of boredom, are in fact a good way to stay physically more alert.
'Studies have shown that children who are allowed to gigo learn more and learn more information than those who are forced to stay without change,' said Professor Fenske.
We're all getting bored one day or another -- the lagging of my airplane flight, a speaker with a single-stroke voice, a particularly borling movie.
But some people are more likely to get bored than others.
To measure this, researchers developed 'Predisposal to Boredom Scale' in the 1980s.
The ladder includes assertions like, "Of the tasks that I do, many of them are repetitive and monotonic," and "I have so many interests that I don't have the time to do everything."
By using such a ladder, the researchers have discovered that boys tend to get bored more often than girls, said Stephen Vodanovich, a professor of psychology at the University of West Florida, among other things in need of numerous and varied stimulation.
But in general, oysters are hudded quickly.
In 1991, Reed Larson, an Individual and Community Development Professor at the University of Illinois, conducted an experiment during which he contacted nearly 400 teenagers and their parents seven to eight times a day via a beeper.
He noted that 32 per cent of the teenagers reported being bored at school and during their homework, while 23 per cent reported being bored when they were not in school.
On the other hand, 3 per cent of parents reported being bored.
Prof. Larson stated that he did not know whether today's boredom levels, 21 years Later, would be higher or lower.
But he added that he was aware that "Adolescence is a time when boredom reaches its highest level", especially because children and adolescents do not have much control over what they want to do.
If I repeat my question: is boredom good for you?
Occasion not, because in its extreme form, it can cause people to take absurd physical risks, make bets or abuse certain substances to pass the time, as the studies show.
On the other hand, many philosophers and writers evoke a link between boredom and creativity, said Professor Vodanovich, who has been studying the problem for more than two decades.
'Be boredom is the way the brain has found to let you know that you should do something else,' said Gary Marcus, a psychology professor at N.Y.U.
But the brain don't always know what is the most appropriate thing to do.
If you get bogged down and use that energy to play the guitar and cook, it will make you happily.
But if you look at the TV, it will make you happy in the short term, but not in the long run.
So if your child gets bored and you give him an iPad, he won't be bored anymore, but he won't have learned to take care of himself or self-trip, says Professor Fenske.
And 'this self-regulation goes from one situation to another,' he said.
Your child doesn't just learn to entertain, he gets more self-proficiency in other areas.
I don't think we should glorify boredom.
Nor do I believe that we should criticize it either.
Feeling uncomfortable outside the constant bubbling generated by activities and technology is the goal that we should strive for.
Professor Eastwood agrees on this.
'We need to annoying ourselves more often, but annoying an acute and frenetic desire to be connected to something that makes sense,' he said.
According to him, what people are looking for is a way of disconnecting and appreceiving stopping times.
"In an environment where we're constantly hyperstimulated," he added, "it's hard to find ways to go into something when the noise stops."
