Petronella Wyatt: I was bullied and excluded from Oxford, because I was a "Tory" (conservative).
It's not just the students of today who are attacked for their opinions.
As far back as I can remember, I have always dreamed of entering Oxford University.
My eldest Father and Brother studied in what I was an eminent place of knowledge, a kind of modern-day Greek amphitheatre that stimulates the two pillars of civilization, free thought and tolerance.
However, two weeks after staying at Worcester College in the late '80s to study history, I decided to pack my bags, provoking the first scandal in my life.
My father collapsed and started crying.
My friends were baffled.
The Evening Standard newspaper claimed that I had left this university, because I had objected the fact that undergraduate students had sex in the bedroom next to mine.
Author A. N.W. Wilson made facely statement that I left after being Forced to Drunk Cups.
The truth was less funny.
I ran away.
Yes, I fled because I had been bullied and harassed.
It wasn't kind of because of my unparalleled name, not because I came from a private school.
I was persecuted for a single reason, and in this cradle of so-called knowledge, I was the victim of racist and barbaric behaviour: my dear Father, Woodrow Wyatt, was a very influential adviser to Margaret Thatcher and I was a supporter of the Conservative Party.
"While we talked about this today," you might say.
Well, recent studies have revealed that a new generation of center-to-right students are experiencing the same kind of persecution.
The increasing and institutionalized hatred of Tory students in Oxford is such that last week, some of them demanded protection of their rights on an equal footing with those of gays, people with disabilities and ethnic minorities.
Curators of the Student Hall (JCR) at Corpus Christi College stated that they were "often isolated, attacked personally and felt undesirable" because of their political views.
They wanted there to be a representative in the University Committee for Equal Opportunities so that their views could be freely expressed.
Their situation has not been facilitated by the recent BBC documentary "Wonderland: Young, Bright and on the Right", which was devoted to student politics, which depicted eccentrics and neo-Nazis.
Student Joe Cooke, former president of the Oxford University Conservative Association (OUCA), who traveled to Rolls-Royce, worn a silver-compartment and moved with a chrome-leading cane.
In other universities, conservative students say they are treated as scapegoats in setting up increased tuition fees."
Luke Black, a 20-year-would vice-president of the Nottingham University Conservative Association, told a Sunday newspaper "there is a growing left bias in universities.
People think we're like the Bullingdon Club without even having met us."
Samuel Roberts, a 21-year-old history student at Corpus Christi, who proposed a motion for further protection, says that such an atmosphere is "uncomfortable," and Stephanie Cherill, a 19-year-old president of OUCA, says that there has been a deterioration of JCR members' attitudes towards people who are centre-right.
'This constitutes an obstacle to freedom of intellectual debate, as well as to the well-being of members,' she said.
I was the only one to think like that in my first few weeks at Oxford.
I came in September 1986, when I was 18 years old and stubbornly timid.
Hate of the conservative party was very virulent.
In the previous year, the university had voted a refusal to give Margaret Thatcher – a former student – an honorary title, due to the reduced funding for higher education.
The atmosphere was loaded with Stalinist vibrations.
From the very first days of the welcoming week, when the new students got to know each other and the faculties, I had a taste of what was going to happen afterwards.
I've come to the conclusion that professors not only participated in sarcasm towards Tory students, but that in addition they enjoyed it.
It was as if the policy on striking miners, privatization and the government's opposition to sanctions against apartheid in South Africa were engrabbed in the timber of classrooms.
The first sarcasm to which I was a victim concerned a translation into English of the French texts of the 18th century, and I was unprepared.
"Miss Wyatt," said Professor Harry Pitt (receivably deceased), "please translate the first paragraph."
I lost my foot.
Pitt, a pussy-faced little-hearted man, carried away easily.
"Did the Thatchedists refuse to learn French or are they just stupid?" he asked.
The other students laughed.
Tears were burning my eyes.
'I suggest you take French lessons during your free time - at last, if you're not too busy making friends for yourself," he said in a snarling tone.
I went back to my inconsolable room.
At dinner that evening, I sailed down alone and then I felt a slight tap on my shoulder.
It was a second-year English student, named James, who introduced himself as a member of OUCA.
"I don't know who you are," he kindly said.
I'm scared that this is the case.
Anyone suspected of being a Tory (conservative) is targeted.
It's hard enough already for me, but they know that your father is close to Margaret Thatcher, so it will be even harder for you.
Most Tory youth presense to be Labour (Labour).
Later, at the local pub, I cowardly tried to hide.
I stressed the fact that I did not agree with everything Mrs Thatcher said.
This ploy has failed.
A first-year student of Philosoosophical Philosophy, who ironically studied in Eton, said, "You are the daughter of a fascist pork."
You're confacturous.
Other students regained the chorus.
I was perverted, dirty.
"Why do the Tories make love?" asked one of them.
They hit each other, don't you?"
I felt like homosexuals before the liberal legislation of the 1960s.
Would I have only been able to lead a normal life in Oxford?
Had I been forced to meet people who share the same points of view only after nightfall?
Had I had to become Labour and deny my opinions?
The next three years would look reminiscent of ostracism and inesligibility.
Tory the only openly Professor Tory was Norman Stone, Professor of Modern History, who was teaching at my university.
He was hated not only because he was a conservative, but also because he was Thatcher foreign policy adviser and one of the writers.
It was almost never there.
He hated this inconstituous and inconstituent place, as well as its Marxist-Deterministic view of history.
In 1997, he accepted a professorship at Bilkent University in Ankara, Turkey.
"You won't be happy here," he told me.
I started shuttling between Oxford and my parents' house in London, siewing refuge with my family and more open-minded burial friends.
I told my dad that I huddled Oxford and told him why.
It was incredulous.
During his studies at Oxford in the 1940s, all political views were accepted.
"But it's the best place in the world," he told me pathetically.
They wouldn't do that, not in dreamy bell tower city.
Even my communist friends had impeccable ways.
He had tears in his eyes.
Give them a chance.
I'm sure it's to tease.
It would break my heart if you left Oxford.
Tired by my frequent trips to London, my emotional resistance gradually deteriorated.
A friend, also Tory, succumbed to the pressure and renounced his convictions.
In a work-leading session the following week, when another professor of history suggested most seriously in the world that I was "an enemy of the people", I decided to do the same.
Robbing disbeliently ashamed, I admitted to being the brainwashing of my parents and calling them "old mad."
The respite was short-lived.
It wasn't my drunkard the last nail in my oxfordian coffin.
At that time, he wrote two columns in Murdoch press every week.
My door was locked.
I lagged into my bedroom, and after five minutes, my pursuers gave up.
When they left, I packed my suitcase and took the first train to London.
I've never gone back.
You can call me a wet helmet.
However, no children between the ages of 18 should be subjected to such intimidation in a school.
The most tragic is that it was Oxford, which not only produced 14 Tory prime ministers, but also enjoyed an unshakeable reputation for equality and freedom of thought.
