He sailed at Countess Jacqueline de Ribes, one of my great friends.
"It was impeccable, years have no effect on him."
If it is a princess who says it...
The world's toughest profession: Kawah Ijen human mules
For four euros, Indonesian volcano wearers are risking their lives and health by carrying 70 kilograms of bulb through the steep stone trails.
There are people for whom work is hell and others who literally work in hell.
This is the case of Anto Wijaya, one of the 400 miners who make their living by carrying the sulfur of the Kawah Ijen volcano to the east of the Indonesian island of Java.
It must work every day to the bottom of the crater, where the sulphide gas emanating from entrails of the earth solidifies in contact with the air.
After grabbing large sulphur rocks, which weigh a total of nearly 70 kilograms, installed them in two bamboo baskets that he loaded on his shoulders through steep trajectories.
There is only 250 meters to the top of the volcano that rises to an altitude of 2.386 meters, but exhausted porters take more than 40 minutes to climb this distance, at a turtle-shape, keeping the balance and measuring their steps so as not stumbling and falling into the precipice.
They know that a misstep could cost them their lives, as happened to a French tourist who made a mortal fall in the Kawah Ijen volcano several years ago.
Miners at Kawah Ijen earn 5 euro cents for every kilogram of sulfur extracted.
Once on the top, it makes their way between the tourists who photograph them as if they were circus beasts, and painfully carrying heavy baskets, they walk the 3 kilometers to the balance that a mining company has set up a bit below 1.850 meters above sea level.
These are PT Candi Ngrimbi, a company that has been operating the volcano since 1960 and, it can be said, its employees, to whom it pays 662 Indonesian rupees (5 euro cents) per kilogram of sulfur.
It then sells it to the petrochemical industry of 10,000 rupees (83 euro cents) since this ore is very much present in everyday life and is used to manufacture candles, fireworks, cosmetics, dynamite and is even used to bleach sugar.
'Since we're transporting about 70 pounds, we're earning about 46,000 rupees (3.38 euros) on each trip,' Anto tells us, who usually makes three trips per day.
Everyone takes him 3 hours and he finishes exhausted, but this allows him to earn 138,000 rupees (11.50 euros) at the end of the day.
Even though it appears to be misery for such a superhuman effort, it's three times what it would earn in the fields.
'The income of miners is very high here, where coffee harvest is paid 10,5000 (EUR 1.20) per day and where the average monthly wage is 2 million rupees (167 euros)", says the wearer who once worked as a mason on the tourist island of Bali.
There, his salary was 75,000 rupees (6.20 euros) per day and the job wasn't as difficult, but Anto came back next to his family in Banyuwangi, a village close to the volcano, for a reason that in Indonesia is as inconvenience: 'I married a girl from Bali, where they are Hindu, and I've brought her back to Java
Anto suffers from asthma, breathes with difficulty, cough constantly and his eyes are irritated by toxic gases.
At the age of 27, he spent three of his life defying the danger on Kawah ljen, but bulb has already begun to make him pay the bill even though it covers its face with a mask and wears special gloves.
Anto suffers from asthma, breathes with difficulty, cough constantly and his eyes are irritated by the toxic gases released by the volcano.
This is the price he has to pay to pursue his dream.
"I'm going to work for two more years, because I want to open a shop or study Spanish or French," he promised in more than acceptable English.
Muded by life, this sympathetic and intelligent young person could be a tourist guide, a wait-and-were or receptionist in a hotel, but, instead, it does the work of a mule.
It shares an unsanitary wooden masure with other wearers, rise every day at two o'clock in the morning because the sulfur never stops grazing at night, when its characteristic yellow color turns blue and shines in the middle of the darkness.
Defying the obscurity, Anto goes into the crater by illuminating with a small lamp attached to his helmet, which he bought himself with his own money.
Some 400 wearers loaded the bulb baskets onto their shoulders from the bottom of the crater.
Despite its cosy profits, the mining company has not mechanized the extraction of ulsulphur to save costs and does not provide any equipment to wear-to-taking carriers and are paid by weight.
In addition, they also do not touch one of the 30,000 rupees (2.50 euros) of camera increase, in addition to 15,000 rupees (1.20 euros), which the guards of this natural park charge tourists who come to photograph the volcano and human mules.
"This work is done for beasts, not for humans," Madrusin protests, a 42-year-old corpulent bearer who has been working in Kawah Ijen for thirty years, since he left the college.
Capable of carrying up to 110 kilograms, he assures that he will not stop working "as much as he can", as he needs money to raise his 3 sons, aged between 10 and 18.
I won't take my retiring, I will die here because the volcano is my whole life.
Although sulfur damages the throat and irritates the eyes when the wind turns and traps the miners in the thick columns of smoke coming out of the volcano, they are so hardened that no one complains of suffering from the serious diseases . . .
By equilibrating the cartoon on its back, Unainik, a 53-year-would, can load only 50 kilograms.
Every day his companions extract 15 tons of sulfur from the volcano, that three lorries carry to the Tamansari Warehouse, 18 kilometers away by a goat trail invaded by weeds.
"I won't retire, I'll die here because the volcano is my whole life," says Unainik by opening his mouth, unveiling several missing teeth.
The eldest of his five children, aged 30, also works as a carrier of sulfur.
Time passes, but misery perpetuates from generation to generation one of the most difficult occupations in the world: that made by human mules of the Kawah ljen volcano.
Search for Babies to Save the Economy
Singaporeans placed responsibility for the lack of children on career, stress and the cost of access to home ownership.
"Sanniest's population must develop".
I'm a husband and a patriot, you're my worry and a patriot, let's fulfil our civic duty and give life.
It may seem implivable that these lyrics came from an advertisement for mint pellets, but despite that, or perhaps because of that, the video spread like wildfire on YouTube in Singapore at the beginning of this year.
The ly lyric comes from a rap group that uses local references such as "Let's put an policolon in the drawer" to make fun of the Singapore Birth Rate.
BBH, the advertising agency at the origin of the video, hopes to draw attention to the problem in a funny way through this advertisement.
Its creative director, Douglas Hamilton, has stated that he wants to use the power of the music so that people fulfil "national duty".
It is intended only for the Internet, so we were able to make it entertaining and entertaining.
This is the main problem in that country.
We are the worst in the world in terms of the birth rate, so we felt that this was a topic that we had to address.
We knew that the government had tried a number of things such as pheromonomy fragrances or organizing speed-dating parties.
Many of these ideas were creative, but they didn't necessarily work.
So we talked to each other the following: what could be more creative to solve this problem than turning it into a rap song?
1.2 children
The government of Singapore does not take it so lightly.
It spends USD 1,300 per year on policies to encourage its citizens to have more children.
A government measure in favour of weddings and fathers granted up to 15,000 dollars per child, extended maternity vacations and distributed tax profits.
But all of this had little effect.
Singapore is a wealthy, high-tech city of South-East Asia, also known for the conservatism of its rulers and strict social controls.
Singapore’s birth rate, in accordance with its national population distribution, currently stands at 1.2 children per woman.
The last time it stood above 2, considered the turnover rate, was in 1976.
So why didn't Singaporeans have more children?
Tan Wei Ming, Director of Wedding and Family Policy of the National People's Division, said it was due to "better education" and "a wider choice of career opportunities".
'This gave people a wider range of options in terms of life objectives and priorities, beyond marriage and the formation of a family,' she explains.
These changes in social norms have contributed to an increase in the number of unmarried persons and to the delay of marriage and births, resulting in a decrease in the birth rate in Singapore.
In the meantime, an immigration policy aimed at drastically increasing immigration in order to counter the decline in the population has created resentment among the local population.
In Singapore, there are websites that acquiesthetically disgusted xenophobia against new immigrants, especially those who are accustomed to drawing wages downwards and unintegrating.
Raising immigration is also seen as one of the reasons why the party operating in Singapore last year experienced its worst electoral results since independence.
Since the elections, they have tried to correct the problem with the introduction of higher quotas and taxes for foreign workers.
Unexpected Consequences
While the fall in the birth rate has known effects on a country’s economic growth, tax revenues, health costs, and immigration policies, Singapore’s example also has unintended consequences.
The government tries to avoid the construction of many small houses.
For example, this has started to affect the real-estate sector.
Urban development authorities have begun to monitor the number of small apartments, known as a "shoebox", which can be built in specific areas of the city.
These apartments have an area of 46 square meters and are very successful.
However, the authorities were preoccupied with the fact that they could promote a single lifestyle and discourage developers who wished to build large family houses.
But Lim Yew Soon, Managing Director of Electural Company EL Developers, claims that its "shoeboxes" sell much faster than larger homes.
They are more popular, in the sense that these homes are built much faster than larger ones.
For this reason, it is much more profitable for our treasury.
However, he concedes that the new standards give clearer instructions to developers, who used to put a stand in the wheel if they wanted to offer too many small dwellings in a project.
Too much Stress
Swriting is a city-state.
While these new regulations can be a step towards an increase in the national birth rate, by talking to Singaporeans who are working in the central financial plaza, it would seem that they do not have much impact.
'People are very stressed, houses are expensive, just like education, that's why people put it off to a future,' says a young executive.
Other people may have children.
'But for me, it is important to be able to dispose of my money and my time,' says another young man in my twenties.
Men and women mention their careers, the stress and the cost of access to property and education as well as the reasons preventing them from owning children.
Thus, although the government was trying to convince its citizens to have children, when it came to making babies, it was the Singaporeans themselves who had the last word.
What's private offline is private online
Confidentiality
In accordance with the Dictionary of the Royal Spanish Academy, this is the quality of the private domain or 'the field of private life that we can protect from intrusion'.
What belongs to the private domain for those under the age of 16?
How to apply this definition in everyday life and on social networks?
Does it understand the dangers to which it is exlapsing events on the Internet that it would certainly not share off-line?
The newspaper elPeridico interviewed five young boys, aged between 10 and 15, frequent users of the canvas.
In four of the cases, they associated the term "something very personal" with "the password and username" when they applied it to social networks.
"I won't unvellate my most personal secrets in a post," says Jorge, ten years, when he tries to explain the meaning of the private term on sites such as Facebook, Twitter, Hotmail and Windows Live Messenger, where he has owned an account for two years.
'These are very secret secrets, my mother can get to know them but not everyone," he says.
On FB, I post cool pictures or games.
I also talk with my friends.
'I won't share one of my pictures or someone who would make the embecile,' he said.
The young boy acknowledges that it is wrong to publish obscene photographs, naked people, crimes or writing humiliating or violent comments.
Jorge assures that he knows his 35 friends on FB as well as his nine followers on tweeting.
The majority are of the family.
Its mother is part of it, she knows the password of one of the accounts.
I've opened a twitter account to express and write interesting tweets.
'I don't know if they're answering me, I'm just posting them,' he adds.
'Social networks are amutable, I can talk to distant relatives or with my friends quickly,' he says.
It states that it would never accept the request of an unknown person.
Nor will it trust someone who would recommend a stranger.
The case of José, aged 14, is different.
The teenager has accounts on Hotmail (Face, Facebook, My Space and Ask), he admits not to know about 20 of the people on his list of friends.
'It doesn't wrinkle me because we have something in common, music for example,' he says.
According to the young boy, no one made advances or asked him for his address or his telephone number.
'While they put the pressure on me or demanded it, I'd just take them out of my account,' he admits.
José subscribed to Ask after locating a recommendation on tweeting.
The experiences known today as cyberharassment are no stranger to the boy.
A relative of a friend of mine was racketeered on a social network.
They threatened it and demanded money.
'He didn't ever know who it was,' he says.
The victim, according to José, did not close his account.
"He only put it in a private profile."
It then explains a series of manipulations to configure its account in a secure way.
Unreagnizant of Jorge, this young person would post photos of knowledge in uncomfortable or embarrassing situations.
Yes, I'd do it if I was angry or angry.
'While I know it's cyberbullying,' he says.
Key issues
Marielos Porras, Professor of English and Bachelor in Education and Apprentice, believes that, in order to orient children and adolescents, it must be understood that the purpose of social networks is to provide information.
'The Internet has emerged as a way of searching for information, but, with the appearance of these sites, the rule of the game has changed,' she says.
Porras points out that Academiologist Marc Prensky, who holds a master's degree in pedagogy from the Yale University and author of the book 'Natives and Digital Immigants', uses these terms to explain the phenomenon.
Digital natives are children and young people who were born with technology.
'We are the digital Immigrants who have to teach them there, while we ourselves are learning,' she says.
It goes on to say that the topic is complex, 'because we are asking them to have a defined criterion on whether or not to divulge, to publish or to say at an age in which they do not have the necessary maturity to do so.'
'We're also asking them to be selective when what matters most to them is to be popular, to have thrifty friends without thinking about the consequences,' she adds.
According to the specialist, the most effective way of teaching confidentiality to children and young people is to use thought-provoking questions.
'Regifting to say not to do so is of no use,' she says.
Second, Porras lists a few examples: there are things you wouldn't say to a stranger, why do you do it on the Internet?
Or, would you like them to publish a picture of you like that of a friend you published?
Do you know what the others publish about you?
When you pick up festivities, do you ask other people for permission to identify them?
And also, does everyone need to know what you're doing every moment?
Another point is to make it clear to them that they need to act online as they do offline.
These are the same rules.
'Ofter the Internet, everyone must act with respect, morality and other principles, it must be the same on social networks,' she says.
Monitoring
Estuardo Guardia, a university professor, a primary schoolteacher and an education counselor, said that it was essential for parents to conscientiously read the conditions for using social networks.
By affluing each word, they will have a solid foundation to discuss with their children the implications of opening an account on the Internet.
'For example, age and what is allowed to share or publish,' he says.
According to Guardia, it was essential to remind children that foreigners should not be told.
The Unasur summit ended without the publication of the Lima Declaration
The Sixth Presidential Summit of the Union of South American Nations (Unasur) ended today in Peru without making the Lima Declaration public, even though it was previously announced and theoretically adopted by the seven trustees present.
Efe has repeatedly tried to gain access to the document signed at the Sixth Ordinary Meeting of Unasur Heads of State and Government, but the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and the Presidency initially reported that it would be leaked after the end of the summit and then stated that it would be published at some point on the website of the Peruvian Government.
When questioned about the text, they pointed out that its content had been explained by the President of Peru, Ollante Humala, in brief statements to the press.
Journalists' access to information about the summit was constantly restricted.
During the course of the day, only the video without the sound of the presidential assembling with the "Private, Restricted Intersession" message was broadcast in the press room.
The lack of information circulated among the journalists was that given by the press officers of some of the Unasur Governments who attended the rendezvous, with the exception of the Peruvian spokesman.
The only document unleashed during the day was the list of presidents present, causing some unease among the hundreds of journalists of various national and international media demanding more information.
Later the Peruvian presidency sent an e-mail to the medieval e-declaration of the summit with "final declaration", but this was President Humala's speech rather than the official document that concluded the summit.
Last October, Peru hosted the Third Summit in South America-Arab countries (ASPA) and, on that occasion, despite the fact that it had been practically requested by the press, it had also not issued the so-called Lima Declaration, as had previously been announced.
It can be verified on the official ASPA website that the document was published last March.
In the two international meetings, the Peruvian authorities had tried to put in place safe transmission systems for all the journalists, but they limited the availability of information as much as possible.
The summit also concluded with the joint agreement between Chile and Peru to accept a judgment of the Court in The The Hague which rules a different border between the two countries.
Presidencies of Peru, Ollanta Humala, and Chile, Sebastian Piaera, met during the regional meeting and stated that they would respect the ICJ's decision to hear the pleadings of the two parties in the Lima lawsuit against Santiago, which begins on Monday in The The Hague.
"We will respect and implement the verdict that will rescind the differences that we are dealing before this international court today", said President Humala, alongside his Chilean counterpart.
'Chile has always been and will continue to be a country that respects international law, the peaceful resolution of controversies, treaties and the international courts", added President Piaera, giving President Humala a handshake, alongside the flags of the two countries.
The confirmation of the two presidents that they would submit to the ICJ took place after Colombia this week denounced the Bogota Pact, by which it had agreed to comply with the decisions of this international tribunal, following a decision on the maritime boundaries with Nicaragua, which it considered to be seriously mistaken.
The summit was held without the presence of the Presidents of Brazil, Dilma Rouseff of Venezuela, Hugo Chavez, Bolivia, Evo Morales and Argentina, Cristina Kirchner.
Paraguay, suspending Unasur since 2011, following the impeachment of its ex-President Fernando Lugo, did not take part in the appointment.
The host Chairman, Ollanta Humala, was instructed to open the deliberations in the morning and to close the summit shortly after no later than no later than noon in Lima.
The trusteeee read the outcome document in which it announced that 16 agreements had been adopted and that action lines of 31 projects had been set between the South American countries for a total of USD 17.0 million.
Among the agreements adopted, he mentioned that the Unasur countries would put in place "important approaches to achieving the goal of South American citizenship through the development of agreements on residence permits".
It informed that action would be put in place to improve "co-operation in the fight against urban insecurity and international organised crime, actions to improve the accessibility of medicines, low-cost Internet access throughout South America and to jointly and effectively assess the risks of natural disasters".
With a Europe in crisis, 'economic (Latin America) economic consolidation must not have a triumphalist matrix but must help to increase our productive matrix and see a better future for our people", concluded President Humala.
'We have decided to favour a group of 31 iconic projects that improve the connectivity of South American spaces, especially in rural and border areas ... by uniting our countries and generating new economic channels,' the Peruvian president said in a speech.
Among those projects, he mentioned that five concerned Peru and were on the transverse axes of its territory, from the coast to Brazil, and that two concerned a larger connection with Equator, although it did not provide further details.
Similarly, the final document refers to the political situation in Paraguay.
'We therefore hope that the electoral process in this country will allow its reintegration into the Union of South-American Nations", from which it is currently excluded.
The need for Latin America to remain an integrated region of prosperity and peace with good relations with its neighbours is another theme discussed at the summit.
In this regard, Colombian President Juan Manuel Santos stated, before starting the regional rendezvous, that he hoped to meet his Nicaraguan counterpart, Daniel Ortega, the following Saturday in Mexico City to calmly discuss the maritime dispute through an ICJ judgment requested by Bogota.
"It's possible that Tomorrow (Saturday), I have a meeting with President Daniel Ortega", said President Santos.
'We are going to revise all these paths, they are not exhaustive, including the Treaty with Nicaragua which will ask for a discussion with this country", he stressed.
"Please be able to tell President Ortega that we are going to settle this in the most civilized and respectful way possible", added President Santos.
Presidents Santos and Ortega will meet on Saturday in Mexico City, where they are scheduled to witness the takeover of the country’s new president, Enrique Pena Nieto.
In the same way, as part of the summit, the bloc's defence ministers will meet in advance to approve the 2013 Action Plan, which seeks to strengthen dialogue and consensus in terms of defence in the region.
Argentina, Bolivia, Colombia, Ecuador, Peru, Brazil, Uruguay, Venezuela, Chile, French Guiana, Suriname and Paraguay are part of Unasur, although it is suspended.
Peru assumes the pro tempore presidency of the regional bloc.
'Southern America must learn from Europe how to integrate a citizenship,' says Rafael Correa.
Equateur President Rafael Correa today stated that the creation of a common citizenship is an objective why 'Southern America, in this case, should emulate Europe.
President Correa, who participated in the Sixth Summit of the Union of South-American Nations (Unasur), located in Lima, stated on state television, TV Péru, that the Europeans 'have started during the Second World's World War and during other conflicts, "while they now form almost a homeland".
To that end, it had defended plans to establish South-American citizenship encouraged by Unasur member countries.
'We must achieve the free movement of citizens and workers in any South American country, as is already the case for the members of the Andean Community, but there are still reactionary areas that we want to make a thing of the past", he warned.
In addition, the President of Ecuador was in favour of rebuilding the Organization of American States (OAS) with the objective of diminishing the influence of Anglo-Saxon States and taking into account the signatories of the San Jose Pact on human rights.
Those rebuffing our ears never compromise each other, conversely we, the South Americans, have all signed.
"It is incomprehensible that the Inter-American Commission for Human Rights is in Washington under the funding of the United States", he said, alluding the political asylum granted by Ecuador to WikiLeak founder Julian Assange.
Correa assures that it does not regret its decision, because it has not itself betrayed its principles, but on the contrary has respected its 'deep democratic and human rights values'.
It concluded that at the time "there were well-founded suspicions that Assange would be extradited to a third country and that his trial would not be respected".
In addition, it criticizes the Swedish justice system, which has demanded an interrogation on an alleged sexual offence in its territory, while "its Swedish legislation allows it to be done via videoconference, which could be done from the Ecuadorian Embassy in London".
Correa stated that there was a risk of Assange's mental and physical health deteriorating.
"I didn't talk to him since he was in our embassy, but the ambassador informed me that he had a small lung problem, but nothing serious," said the President of Ecuador.
Its physical and mental health may deteriorate by being locked up in a small space without being able to exercise in the open air.
'It affects the health of anyone,' he concludes.
Correa points out that Assange's asylum solution since June at the Ecuadorian embassy in London, which would be the attributation of safe conduct that would enable him to travel to Ecuador, is in the hands of Great Britain, Sweden and the European judicial authorities and stressed that he had held talks in London in order to find a solution to the confinement of the founder of WikiLeak.
We don't negotiate with human rights, we don't use that word in this case, but there have been ongoing discussions.
'The solution to this problem lies in the hands of Great Britain, Sweden and the European judicial authorities because Assange lawyer Baltazar Garzon has launched a wide range of proceedings in various European bodies,' he said.
And he thinks that 'if Great Britain gave the duct safe tomorrow, it would come to an end.'
And if Sweden, as its legislation quite allows, and as it has already done in other cases, questioned Mr Assange in the Ecuadorian Embassy in London, or questioned him via Skype tomorrow, the problem would also be solved.
Correa took the opportunity to reassert himself as a defender of freedom of the press and made it clear that what he could not tolerate was "medice, bad faith and lyrnaly that undermines freedom of expression".
'The biggest enemies of freedom of the press are not malicious or perverse politicians, but the bad journalists in favour of greed, blackmail and extortion,' he said.
From this point of view, he welcomed the fact that it was not that kind of journalists "neither the hegemian nor the hegemonic and bourgeois countries that dominate in Equence" and, rather than highlight its re-election, 'he would dripathize the revolution to pursue the path and pave the right track'.
Correa had also supported the decision to maintain the veto over Paraguay within the Unasur at least until the forthcoming elections, arguing that the body "must stand firm and do not tolerate conguised expediency and attempted coup d'état", because in reality "this destroyed the legitimacy of Paraguayan democracy".
What is more, the Ecuadorian President regards as "completely relevant" the desire of his Colombian counterpart, Juan Manuel Santos, to negotiate the maritime boundaries between the two countries with Nicaragua through the judgment of the International Court in The The Hague, which will strengthen the maritime sovereignty of Nicaragua.
For the time being, this judgement is not respected.
It is a problem between a South American country and a Central American country.
Conflicts were inevitable, but they must be overwhelmed by the desire to move forward together.
They as a whole have to be resolved to go beyond them and move forward.
At the same time, he expressed confidence in the proper resolution of the dispute concerning the maritime borders between Peru and Chile within the same tribunal and stated that it was "beneficial that Latin America should seize the international authorities if the two countries agree to a judgement that is so firm".
With reference to the possibility of his presentation as a candidate for the future presidential elections in Equator, with a view to wrangling for a third consecutive term of office, he pointed out that he saw this possibility "with a great deal of optimism and joy, although it is sometimes quite difficult".
Correa assured that, if he lost the primaries in February 2013, he would withdraw from public life.
At the personal level, power has never interested me, but in situations as unfair as those of Ecuador, this socio-economic poverty can be resolved only through political power.
'My political movement has believed that it is me that guarantees this possible victory, but we have to accept this responsibility,' he said.
If I won, this would be my last presidential term, and then I will step out of political life.
If we ever get lost, I'll do it as well.
"This is my decision," he said.
Correa also referred to the new medical treatment of the Venezuelan president, Hugo Chavez, in Cuba.
I have just discussed with Venezuelan Vice-President Nicolas Maduro and he told me that routine medical treatment is already planned and that he is awaited the end of the campaign to go back to Cuba.
'It doesn't mean a fall in the state of health of President Chavez,' he commented.
The Ecuadorian Head of State participated today in the Sixth Summit of Heads of State and Government of the Union of South American United Nations (Unasur), which ended with a call for better regional integration in order to support progress, equality and security.
AIDS-related deaths are now due to late detection.
Fabrizio was 21 years of age when it was confirmed the result of the HIV-positive test.
'It's as if heaven had fallen on my head,' he said at the time of the announcement, that the doctor had tried to make 'less painful' in vain.
The young man hid it from his family.
He decided to take care of his illness alone and began to find out about it and his zeal was such that he just celebrated its 43rd anniversary.
Undoubtedly, it is one of the oldest patients in the Guadalajara Civilian Hospital (HCG), in which it ended up in 1994 after numerous battles.
Fabrizio had been living with human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) for 22 years, something difficult to conceive in the early '90s, where there was a lot of doubts, little choice of treatment and greater stigma.
Then, even the director of an IMSS Clinic avoided saying goodbye "because he was voiceless."
At that time, having AIDS was synonymous with dying.
Nowadays it is possible to survive this syndrome and to have a good quality of life.
However, unaware of their disease, many people still arrive when the virus has already caused damage, “exhausted” their immune system and are victims of opportunistic infections.
31 years after the appearance of AIDS in the world, in any case after the first reported cases, 'the great victory today is that the survival of a patient who has started treatment at the right time and the survival of the population in general is exactly the same,' said HCG HIV unit leader Jaime Andrade Villanueva and he takes as a reference that this information has been validated in April this year.
Infectiologist and HIV/Immunity expert, Andrade Villanueva, commented that since 2008, the scientists had concluded that AIDS was no longer a fatal disease, but that the number of years of survival and quality of life depended on the level of assignment of the immune system presented by patients at the time of diagnosis, with better prospects for those not consuming drugs: up to 30 years for a CD4 rate of up to 50 years.
Simply speaking, this means that for someone who receives a positive HIV diagnosis at the age of 25, 'as long as he remains under control can live without problems until the age of 75,' explained the scientist interviewed.
In order to put this progress in its context, it should be borne in mind that the life expectancy of Mexicans today averages 76 years.
While mortality has decreased significantly in recent in recent years, in the case of Mexico, the number of people who died as a result of AIDS fell from 6,678 in 2007 to 4,862 in 2011 (according to the UN/AIDS annual report), it is also adjunct since the appearance of AIDS, 60 per cent of patients on the basis of national data have died.
In Jalisco alone, 255 people died in 2011 and there had been 187 deaths until May this year; however, we are assured that access to anti-retroviral drugs has been universal since 2005.
So why are there still deaths?
I don't think the problem comes from access to treatment.
That's what I can see, this is what happened in our hospital.
Over at least the last 12 years, we have never run out of drugs, the problem is that patients arrive at very advanced stages because they don't know that they're infected, or they're learning it at advanced stages of the disease.
Indiscriminable fact, "Neus out of ten patients arrive while they already have an opportunistic infection, what it is necessary to have a greater impact on overall mortality is to carry out earlier diagnoses and, to do this, it is necessary to propose massive screening tests to all those who request it".
The specialists and members of the State Council for the Prevention of AIDS of Jalisco (Coesida) agree on its proposal, as do the patients themselves, such as Fabrizio, who had to test his screening in a private lab, simply because a friend did so and, despite his young age, he was already a carrier of AIDS and suffered from Kaposi sarcoma, a cancerous tumor.
Everything changes when you learn that you have AIDS.
Some people think they're going to die and don't want to know anything.
'If I'm going to die, it's better for me to have fun three times a week," they say, "isn't it."
The change has been beneficial, I'm eating well, I'm doing exercise, I'm taking my medication.
To this day, his parents only know that he suffers from cancer.
I have a normal life, like anyone else.
"I work, I have a lot of activities, I travel, I have an active sexual life, but co-responsible, I pay attention to myself and the other person," says Fabrizio, who agreed to share his intimacy with MILENIO JALISCO to help, thanks to his testimony, those who are scared today at the World AIDS Day march.
Let them do the test, if they have taken risks, the sooner they will know if they are HIV-positive, and the better, and if they already have the diagnosis, they must know that we can live like everyone else, by being responsible.
Its message summarises the slogan of the fight against AIDS in the year 2012.
Confucuses on the counter
There are many gaps between health programmes and the ordinary citizen, supports Ricardo Salazar, a Guadalajara journalist who blunted the cause of HIV.
And the greatest is prevention.
In all the places dedicated to this, 'the distribution of condoms has indeed been increased, before we were given one or two, now we are given packets of a hundred, which is all very good, but as a result, even today, some people don't have access to the condoms,' he says.
Among those most vulnerable to new infections are adolescents.
The question: "What are you going to do about it?", raised with sarcasm and value judgements by social workers, guidance counsellors, pharmacists and healthcare professionals, is the question that teenagers don't want to hear, says the journalist.
It proposes to re-revolt this instaining distribution; that condoms should not only be sold at counters, but should not only be found in public toilet dispensers and in places frequented by young people.
It is not about encouraging promiscuity.
It is also not a question of paying the girls and the motel, as Governor Emilio Gonzalez replied when he was asked whether there would be a distribution of condoms within his administration.
'This is not how sexuality works, it's best to provide condoms for people who are already sexually active,' he said.
Figures in Jalisco
There are 13.435 cumulated cases (12,158 of AIDS and 1.317 HIV).
The State is the country's fourth largest number of new and cumulative cases of AIDS and the 13th largest number of people living with HIV.
92% of infections occur sexually, 6% via blood and 2% perinatal.
It is estimated that 50,000 people are living with HIV, since for each registered case there are 4 or 5 people who do not.
A judgment ratified on appeal by a U.S. court ignores Vitro Group's debt restructuring through commercial bankruptcy in Mexico; the culminating a negative precedent for all domestic companies with branches in the neighboring country with solvency problems.
It therefore seemed that the procedures for the survival of companies permitted by Mexican laws were not valid in the country with a star-studded banner, contrary to international agreements.
From a practical point of view, the blow to the judgement handed down on June 15 by Judge Harlin Hale of the Bankruptcy Court for the Northern District of Texas leaves Mexican companies defenceless in the face of the possibility of embargoing their properties beyond Bravo.
However, the resolution will allow the major glass manufacturer in Mexico to refer the Supreme Court of the United States, based on three inconsistencies.
First of all, while the case judge points out that creditors must register under the United States Bankruptcy Act, the New Orleans Fifth Circumstance Appeals Tribunal asserts that the main procedure is formalized commercial bankruptcy in Mexico.
The first point would involve denunciation of international procedural co-progress cooperation in cases involving companies with a cross-border profile.
In fact, the United Nations Model Law for the Uniformity of International Commercial Law was created for this purpose by using the American Law Institute as an arbiter.
As a second step, the judgment establishes that, without inter-companies vote, recognised in the critical mass of commercial bankruptcy, Vitro's debts owed by subsidiaries towards the parent company, the majority required for the approval of the restructuring could not have been obtained.
However, Mexican laws recognize this possibility.
In fact, Vitro is not the first to use this plan.
Half-twelree examples here, including Agremex and Comercial Mexicana, which the Federal Institute of Commercial Faillits guaranteed the debts.
In addition, what is certain is the fact that, since the votes of the subsidiaries remained, in every way Vitro creditors who fought against this in the courts of the United States, i.e. "vover" funds such as Aurelios Capital, Aurelios Convergencia, Elliot Internacional and Liverpool Limited would not have obtained a majority.
Voted by 45 votes to 37.
A datum omitted by the Court of Appeal.
On the other hand, Vitro was blamed for the difficult situation it had encountered since 2008 without stopping at the serious economic crisis facing the United States, passing on the costs to the country.
For the time being, the Gonzalez Sada society will appeal to the same appellate court to ensure that the vote takes place in its entirety, i.e. with the five judges, since only three had voted.
The insurance claim will demand non-prosperity and requires the review of the trial by a superior court, in this case the United States Supreme Court of Justice.
The most serious thing is that the court ignored a document sent by the Government of Mexico on behalf of amicus curiae ("Friend of the Court"), detailing Vitro's procedure under the Commercial Bankruptcy Act, pointing out that this freed it from the conventions signed by the two countries to the same level as paragraph 15 of the United States Bankruptcy Act.
In addition, he pointed out that the country had complied fully with the principles of the United Nations Commission on International Trade, i.e. rules for cross-border insolvencies, which guaranteed fairness for debtors and creditors.
Carboating of two parts: they stink Vitro and they stutter the country.
Overall stocktaking
Competences by the trade unions of the Société Mexacienne d'Aviation against ex-boss of the company, Gaston Azcoraga Andrade, were ignored for several months, he was accused of fraudulent administration; the Association Syndicale des Pilotes Aviateurs has already encountered problems.
The case, headed by Carlos Daaz Chavez Morineau, has just submitted a criminal application against the National Banking and Valuation Commission which is charged with obstructing the courts.
In his view, the inspectorating authority systematically denied providing information to the Attorney-General of the Attorney-in-Office of the Attorney-in-Office of the Attorney-General of the Public Prosecutor's Office in connection with an operation carried out by the gambler company in connection with the trust F/589 from the IXE bank in the name of Mexicana de Aviacion.
The funds are said to have been used for the purchase of shares from the Hotel Professional Administration.
As is well known, Andrade is the main shareholder of the Posadas hotel chain.
They besieged the Dragon Mart
Joining weekend at an amphitheatre of the University of the Caribbean, a group of national and foreign ecologists, university professors, entrepreneurs and members of civil society approved the creation of a broad opposition front at the inauguration of the Chinese Dragon Mart in Cancun.
As as you know, we are talking about a colossal sales and distribution center in Mexico, Central America and the Caribbean of products from the Great Wall country, with an accommodation area for the employees of 150 companies.
Previously, Canacintra (the National Chamber of Industry and Transformation) had succeeded in uniting governors in the south-eastern zone in order to oppose the monumental project, which is partly on protected areas and represents the greatest of all threats to industry.
ACTA is dead
The government put forward a senate requirement to explain the terms and circumstances the Mexican Ambassador to Japan signed the Anti-Counterfeiting Trade Agreement, known under its English acronym of ACTA, according to the Mexican Institute of Intellectual Ownership, this topic is already in ancient history.
As we all know, this was done despite the fact that the senate itself disclaimed this possibility, as it was regarded as an infringement of freedom of expression on social networks.
Long-term homex
In an attempt to pay long-term debts without affecting short-term debts, Real Estate Developer Homex embarked on the Stock Exchange for 500 million pesos.
This securities issue is the first of the four issues for which they propose to pay interest every 28 days.
Birth of Competival
A new Competival-based consortium has just been set up by NYCE, e-Quelity and Kernet, a new Competival-driven consortium, the objective of which will be the market for clustering software services in Central and South America.
Investing in this area exceeds 1.5 billion dollars.
Héctor "Hetin" Reyes "Sbasketball is all my life"
"Hetin" Basketball Gloglophator Reyes spent more than 60 years of his life in connection with the field and, thanks to him, traveled all over the world.
Few people in Puerto Rico have knowledge of local basketball history as tall as Hector "Hetin" Reyes.
The reason for this fact is that before a cerebral hemorrhage doomed to the wheelchair in 2008, Reyes was immersed in this sport for more than 60 years, either as an inferior league cheerman, player of the National Basket Basket Team, manager and leader of the national team, the BSN, with the Vaqueros de Bayamon or as President of the Basketball Federation.
"I wore several caps in basket ball throughout my life, and even several at the same time, such as when I was president of the BSN, general manager of the national team and chairman of the federation during the ' 1990s," Reyes recalled during a visit of Primera Hora to his home to Bayamon, where he resided with Isabel, his faithful wife for more than 50 years.
"Sbasketball is my whole life."
Reyes doesn't exaggerate when he makes this statement.
The walls of his house, almost all decorated with photos and memorabilia reminiscent of his unparalleled itinerary, testified.
Bayamon in the Heart
Of all his recollections, those he loved the most were those that reminded him of his appearance as a player in the Vaqueros team in the mid-1950s until 1982, after having spent 15 years as a co-manager or manager of the team.
"This has been my best years, the ones that I took the greatest advantage because I was lucky enough to participate in eight Vaqueros Championships starting in 1967, whether as a manager, co-manager or leader.
It has been many years of satisfaction, including the five championships contested from 1971 to 1975.
I then retired at the Championship in 1981, when Jerome Mincy joined the BSN team.
Starting from this, "Cuco" Ortiz, who was a great trustegross, took command of the team," Reyes said.
I'm reminding myself that Gene Bartow, who had been a leader here and was at the University of Alabama (Birmingham), told me: "I have a tough player for you, measuring two meters.
"Do you want it?"
That's how Mincy started, one of Puerto Rico's best players.
Bartow then recommended Raymond Gausse to me, who was naturalized and who was one of better shooters.
I would like to say that if Mincy allowed Bayamon to win a championship, Gausse was going to help us try to win another.
He rejoiced in the Vaqueros Championship with Gausse, but only at a distance since in 1988 he was already the great manitou of the federation.
At that time, he preferred to take advantage of Mincy's ombudsles and those of Mincy in the national team.
I remember when we first thrilled it in the United States in the 1989 Pre-Olympic tournament in Mexico City.
Then there was the 1990 World Curriculum, where we arrived 4th and where we should have won the bronze medal if this Canadian judge hadn't made us replay the last leg, Reyes said.
Is the 1990 World Curriculum team the best national team you've seen?
It is one of the best, as well as beating Dream Team at the 2004 Olympics.
But my favorite is the Pan-Canadian Games of Cuba in 1991, when we gailed the gold and put a beating on the United States team, which was quite similar to the one that won bronze at the World Curriculum.
This team was not only made up of Mincy, Gausse, Ramon Rivas, Fico Lapez and "Piculin" (Ortiz), there were also the young (Javier) "Toito" Colon and James Carter, Brothers (Francisco and Edgar) and Mario "Quijote" Morales, who had not been able to attend the World's World of 90.
A team that was perhaps not the best in terms of individuals, but it gave us a gold medal and great delight at the pre-Olympic tournament in Neuquén in Argentina.
With Players like "Canito" Nieves, Pablo Alicea and the young Rolando Hourruitiner replacing the players hanging by Mar del Plata Pan-American Games imbroglio, we won the gold against all the prognoses.
What was the best player in Puerto Rico?
Undoubtedly, this is Piculin Ortiz.
Its international tournament stats are impressive.
No one in Puerto Rico dominated at this level, as did Piculin.
Without taking into account his trajectory in the different leagues he played for.
Who was the best Puerto Rican ruler?
It is very difficult to say.
We had a good team of Julio Toro, Flor Meléndez, Carlos Morales, Raymond Dalmau, Armandito Torres.
In young people, I very much like the work that Leo Arill does.
For you, what is your greatest success within the federation?
Being part of the most glorious time of the national team between 1988 and 1995, and the fact that in the early 1990s the BSN numbered up to 17 teams in one season.
What do you still have to do?
There are things that I would have wanted to put in place, like the regionalisation of minor categories.
For example, Ponce's boys play only in their area and only confront teams from other corners of the island during national playoffs.
Nowadays, young people are traveling and cheering too much when this is not necessarily necessary.
At the very least, I have seen the advantage of compulsory qualifications and courses for managers, field staff and referees.
It makes me happily.
What are you doing today?
Typically, I listen to music, watch clip clips of my time on YouTube, I take advantage of my grandchildren and, from time to time, I participate in basketball games.
And of, of course, I take advantage of the company of my wife, Isabel, which has always been by my side.
Actor Larry Hagman
Larry Hagman (born September 21, 1931 in Forth Worth, Texas), became known globally by the lead role of John Ross Ewing, better known as J.R., in the television series "Dallas", in which he embodied an unscrupulous, fearsome and manimanipulative businessman.
Larry Hagman, whose unscrcrupulous oil magnate J.R. Ewing in the television series "Dallas" became a symbol of greed in the 1980s, died.
He was 81 years old.
Hagman, who had reunited this year as J.R in a new "Dallas" season, died of cancer complications on Friday night, according to a family statement sent to The Associated Press for Warner Bros., "Dallas production house".
"Larry was back in his dear hometown of Dallas, once again playing the ironic role he loved the most," said the family.
Larry's family and her close friends were with him in Dallas on this Thanksgiving Holiday.
Linda Gray, who played the role of his worry in the original series and in the sequel, stood with Hagman when he died in a hospital in Dallas, said press officer Jeffrey Lane.
He brought rejoice to all who knew him.
It was creative, generous, funny, loving and affectionate, and I'm going to miss him a lot.
'It was an exceptional person, and he was living life to great length,' Gray said in a statement.
