But Larry Hagman enjoyed his masterful interpretation of the delightfully abhorrent J.R. that Hagman experienced his greatest success.
CBS drama on the Ewing clan and their loved ones aired from April 1978 to May 1991.
The slogan "Who Killed J.R.?", invented to maintain the suspense around an episode full of emotions in which the Hagman character is almost assassinated, generated speculation all over the world and millions of dollars played in bets.
As a result, among other things, the series reached an errance at that time.
When the response was unveiled in an episode in November 1980, 41 million viewers averaged in front of their screens and made "Dallas" the second most watched entertainment program in the world, following the final episode of "MASH" in 1983, which brought together 50 million viewers.
It was the sister-in-in-how-in-in-sister-of-the-famous sister-in-chief Kristin (interpreted by Mary Crosby) who had shot him.
J.R. made her pregnant and then threatened to accuse him of prostitution if she did not leave the city, but other people also had reasons to attack her.
Hagman played the role of Ewing as a corrupt and insatibilizable man with charismatic smile: a dishonest entrepreneur and an infidel husband who was trying to indemnify his alcoholic wives Sue Ellen (Linda Gray).
'I know what it would take as an epitaph for JR,' said Hagman in 1988.
It should be Writing: 'This is the thriving citizen J.R. Ewing.
This is the only party he lost.
Victoria Principal, co-protagonist of the original series, recalled Hagman on Friday as an "greascable man, both on the small screen and in life".
It is unforgivable and irreplaceable for millions of fans around the world, and in the hearts of all of us, who were lucky enough to get to know and love him.
Ten episodes of "Dallas" were aired a few months a few months ago with great success on TNT.
The five-episode recording has already been completed for the second season and a sixth is in the process of being made, informed the channel.
