A "End of the Wreight of the World" course has just begun
Every week, students study apocalyptic themes such as nuclear war, zombies, viruses and germs and global warming.
This semester, when religious professor Stuart Charmé decided to give a lecture on the end of the world, he knew that he was launching an irresistible hook there: the end of "great tally" of the Maya calendar on December 21, to which many people refer as proof of the end of the impending world.
But Professor Charmé didn't have any idea of what awaited him over the next few months: Hur Hur Hur Hur Hurricane Sandy, a fiscal abyss that some have dubbed 'Armageddon of debt' and a growing conflict implicating Israel, the place where end-of-a-time Christian theoreticians thought that the apocalypse would trigger.
"I didn't realize that this was going to be the most apocalyptic semester that I've ever seen," Professor Charmé told his students at Rutgers-Camden University (New Jersey).
Analysing what happened in the world today as if we were 30 days later and we were telling it, it was a very good period.
And we must remember that what is bad is good for those who believe in apocalypse.
And it is not the only teacher to give courses on "the End of the World" this semester, which in theory will be the last in history.
At Temple, Assistant Professor Barry Vacker taught "Media, Culture and the End of the World".
Every week, students study apocalyptic themes such as nuclear war, zombies, viruses and germs and global warming.
'We are analyzing this because these ideas proliferate over time,' he said, and the way they offer hypothetical strategies guides some human behaviour.
If nuclear armament were to fall into the hands of terrorists, for example, a war could shatter.
This month, students will analyze films with apocalyptic themes and study how they can be compared with real-life examples.
"I tried to inform my students about what was possible, likely, credible and impossible," said Vacker.
At Pennsylvania State University’s main campus, Latin American History Professor Matthew Restall and his colleague Amara Solari, an Assistant Professor of Art History and Anthropology, teamed up to give a lesson called “The End of the World”.
'We are adding '2012' to have the opportunity to give the course again, in case the world doesn't disappear,' said Professor Restall.
Despite "imminent disaster", students must study, create projects and take their final exams.
In Pennsylvania, the final exam will take the eve of the Apocalypse, leaving no choice for students other than to work "until the very night of the supposed end of the world," Restall said.
Courses proved to be quite popular.
'It was complete in two hours,' said Restall about its course for high-level students, composed of 35 students.
We received e-mail messages several weeks before the beginning of the six-seam), from people who asked if there was still room.
The students, for their part, ensure that the course is one of the most interesting.
'It fascinates me to see what people are able to do to comfort each other,' says Bridgid Robinson, a 23-year-old student of religion and sociology from Haddonfield, New Jersey, of the University of Rutgers-Camden.
Because the fear of the Apocalypse, whether secular or religious, is only a matter of solace, or absence of comfort.
Will Wekesa, a 25-year-old Psychology and Nursing student, claimed to have watched every apocalyptic film.
'I had never heard of a course that taught this subject,' he said.
I like it.
But none of the students surveyed, much less a professor, told us to believe by the end of the world on 21 December.
'Our first project dealt with Mayan prophecy, and in a way we discredited it,' says Julie zegle, a last-year Temple student, a 21-year-old student from West Chester.
The Maya have never predicted the end of the world; this is only a key date on the calendar, Restall said.
But I conceive that there is an anxiety related to apocalypse in Western culture, dating back several centuries, whereby people are reacting to changes in their environment by preagonizing the end of the world.
The Internet has caused these speculations to flourish.
'In other parts of the world, people don't think about this,' he says.
This is the majority of the Anglo-speaking world.
Joseph Dougherty, a Professor of Religion at La Salle University who is giving classes in the Philippines this year, responded quickly when he was asked if he knew we were giving courses on "The End of the World".
'Philipino is not participating at the end of the world,' he told us, as if it were an exception coming from a higher authority.
The Pope has a grace.
Restall points out that over the years, there has been talk of the many supposed dates of the Last Judgement, and he states that if nothing happens on December 21, "People will immediately start to think about the next date" or philosophizing the fact that 21 December marks the beginning of a period of seven years at the end of which the world will disappear.
Students and faculty take the date lightly.
Several people have told us that they are thinking about "the end-of-the-world" parties.
'Sometimes I'm calling for friends together,' commented Samira Ford, a 20-year-old communications lobbies.
